\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 860 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2012\par 10:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: EU GOVERNMENTS CLOSER TO FISCAL UNION DEAL, EUR500B BAILOUT FUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leaders of 25 European Union governments agreed on what some were billing as a historic pact to move to closer fiscal union and to finally sign off on the details of a EUR500 billion ($660 billion) permanent bailout fund for the euro zone that should come into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at mid-year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JUNCKER: WON'T RULE OUT GREEK HAIRCUT FOR PUBLIC-SECTOR CREDITORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eurogroup head Jean-Claude Juncker said he won't rule out the possibility that public-sector creditors might also have to forego repayments on their loans to Greece.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT UP 4.0% IN DEC, BEATS FORECASTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial production rose 4.0% in December from the previous month, the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry said Tuesday, as the impact of the flooding in Thailand on Japanese manufacturers began to ease.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMANY ADJ UNEMPLOYMENT AGAIN HITS RECORD LOW IN JAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's seasonally-adjusted unemployment fell again in January, beating analysts' expectations, while the adjusted unemployment rate again hit a new record low, data from the country's Labor Agency showed Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN RETAIL SALES FALL IN DECEMBER BUT STILL UP IN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German retail sales fell in December, defying expectations for a rise, but were still higher in the whole of last year as consumer sentiment was buoyed by a strong labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IMF SAYS ASIA HAS ROOM FOR STIMULUS IF OUTLOOK DARKENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia has so far proved resilient to the European sovereign-debt crisis and slowing global growth, and countries in the region have room to take growth-boosting steps in case of a sharp downturn, the International Monetary Fund said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TURKEY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ISSUES HAWKISH INFLATION FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday issued a hawkish inflation report, revising its price forecasts sharply higher and stressing that policy should remain tight to counter price pressures and external risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN AZUMI: TO TAKE DECISIVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister issued a fresh warning Tuesday that he will take "decisive steps" if speculators push the yen up too sharply, after the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose to its strongest level in around three months overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IMF SCHIFF: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHOULD ONLY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF HIGH VOLATILITY \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market during periods of high volatility, a senior official at the International Monetary Fund said Tuesday, adding that such action has little lasting effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA SAYS TO TAME INFLATION EVEN AT EXPENSE OF GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean President Lee Myung-bak Tuesday said the government must anchor inflation even at the expense of weaker growth, placing price stability firmly at the top of the administration's economic priorities even as December's industrial output unexpectedly contracted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 'S GOKARN: PRESSURE PERSISTING ON LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian banking system is still facing liquidity pressures, a top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Tuesday, raising hopes that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  would resume its bond-buyback program to infuse cash into the system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDIA CUTS LAST FISCAL YEAR GDP GROWTH ESTIMATE TO 8.4% FROM 8.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian government Tuesday lowered slightly its estimate of the country's growth in gross domestic product for the last fiscal year ended March 31, 2011, to 8.4% from 8.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA END-SEPTEMBER EXTERNAL FINANCIAL ASSETS TOTALED $4.67 TRILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's total foreign assets came to $4.665 trillion at the end of September, up around 1% from the end of June, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S AUTO EXPORTS -2.1% ON YEAR IN DEC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's exports of cars, trucks and buses declined 2.1% on year in December, falling for the first time in five months, as Japanese car makers sought to cut back exports from their home country amid the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S AUTO PRODUCTION +13.4% ON YEAR IN DEC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Production of cars, trucks and buses in Japan increased 13.4% on year in December, rising for the third consecutive month, as car makers revved up output with their normalized parts supply chains to make up for lost production after the March earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE MAS CHIEF: ASIAN ECONOMIES NEED IMF TO REMAIN ACTIVE IN REGION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian economies need the International Monetary Fund to continue playing an active role in the region and to complement regional financial arrangements, the chief of Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA PBOC DOESN'T HOLD TUESDAY'S OPEN MARKET \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - TRADERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't conduct its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday, in a move apparently aimed at easing tight liquidity conditions in the banking system as a large amount of reverse repurchase agreements will mature this week, traders participating in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA JANUARY MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY LIKELY SLOWED - POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing activity likely contracted in January after a brief rebound in December, as factories and plants sat idle across the country during the just ended weeklong Lunar New Year holiday, economists said. [ 31-01-12 0904GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120131e81v000vp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
