\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Markets hold ranges before U.S. jobs data\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2012\par 01:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * MSCI Asia ex-Japan marginally lower, Nikkei down 0.3 pct\par \par \uc2 * Dollar hovers near record low vs yen\par \par \uc2 * U.S. payrolls data due at 1330 GMT\par \par \uc2 By Chikako Mogi\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Asian shares and major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stuck in ranges on Friday ahead of key U.S. jobs data, which will offer more clues over the state of the world's largest economy, while Greek debt restructuring talks dragged on.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan\par \par \uc2 stood down 0.1 percent, while Japan's Nikkei average opened down 0.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Australian shares edged up, with mining stocks heading higher as news that \uc2 Glencore\uc2  and \uc2 Xstrata\uc2  are in talks on a $80 billion merger stirred interest.\par \par \uc2 European shares closed at a six-month high on Thursday after miner \uc2 Xstrata\uc2  and commodities trader \uc2 Glencore\uc2  confirmed merger talks, helping to lift sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "Basically market players will likely take a wait-and-see stance ahead of U.S. payroll data and as Greece has not reached a debt swap deal yet," said Masayuki Doshida, senior market analyst at Rakuten Securities, adding that \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's China services PMI data could offer some trading incentives.\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers are expected to have added 150,000 jobs in January, compared with 200,000 the month before.\par \par \uc2 Shinichiro Kadota, a strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Tokyo, said since December numbers reflected a temporary rise in part-time jobs during the holiday season, a drop in January back to around 150,000 jobs would still suggest moderate recovery in the labour market.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's data showing new U.S. claims for unemployment benefits falling last week pointed to a better jobs market.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 76.16 yen, keeping near a record low around 75.31 yen set on Oct 31, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar stood steady against the euro at $1.3135.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said on Thursday he was seeing signs that some of the factors dampening U.S. business investment, including uncertainty surrounding European bank woes, might be waning. But he kept the option of further easing on the table.\par \par \uc2 Greece has kept hopes of a deal alive all this week but has pushed back the actual debt swap agreement needed to secure a crucial second batch of funds to prevent Athens from defaulting.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone finance ministers aim to agree a second financing package for Greece on Monday. A deal for Greece would include agreement on official new financing, the size of voluntary losses banks and other private bondholders are willing to accept and new reforms Athens must undertake.\par \par \uc2 Despite a lack of progress in the debt restructuring talks, sentiment continued to improve in European debt markets, which are feeling the effect of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unprecedented funding provisions in December.\par \par \uc2 Europe's interbank lending rates fell further as funding stress eased, while Spain and France drew healthy demand for their debt sales on Thursday, driving their funding costs lower.\par \par \uc2 Asian credit markets were steady, with spreads on the iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment grade index barely changed early on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Brent prices rose for a third straight day on Thursday, supported by cold weather in Europe and geopolitical concerns including the West's standoff with Iran. U.S. crude\par \par \uc2 dropped more than 1 percent, weighed by weak demand and robust supplies in the United States. The price differential between the two contracts widened to near three-month highs.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano in Tokyo; Editing by Richard Pullin)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 1, GRAPHIC)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120203e81u0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
