\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Alert Louder Than Ever\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2012\par 12:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Yen is rising again as G10 yield outlook deteriorates\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may be even more sensitive to yen rise than before\par \par \uc2 --Worries about trade gap as well as more deflation\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alerts should be switched on again.\par \par \uc2 If anything, they should be turned on even louder than before.\par \par \uc2 For a few weeks there, the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsided. The yen had fallen back, much to the relief of Japanese authorities as the global risk environment improved and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost some of its safe haven attraction.\par \par \uc2 However, everything has swung back again in the yen's favor in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 It all kicked off when the U.S. Federal Reserve gave its dovish assessment for the U.S. rate outlook, suggesting that monetary tightening would be delayed until late 2014. The market had been looking for rates to start rising again in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Speculation that the Fed might still resort to further monetary easing has only increased since last Friday's news that annualized gross domestic product growth in the fourth quarter of last year amounted to only 2.8% rather than the 3.0% that had been expected.\par \par \uc2 Fears are that growth in the first quarter of this year will now be even slower if companies start to run down the inventories they built at the end of last year. That contributed as much as 1.9% of the growth figure.\par \par \uc2 As a result first quarter growth may well come in even closer to 2%, analysts at ING Financial Markets said.\par \par \uc2 This deteriorating outlook for U.S. interest rates, with 10-year Treasury yields falling to 1.87% from 2.06% a week ago, coincided with a softer monetary outlook in many other countries in the Group of 10 industrial nations, meaning that Japanese investors will show even less interest in putting their money abroad.\par \par \uc2 Apart from the relative rate outlook, there is also another decline in global market sentiment as Greek debt negotiations are proving even more difficult than expected. The continued, and some may say increased, risk of a Greek default means that the yen's safe haven role will return, making the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to foreign investors than it has been over the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 But, there are two reasons to believe that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , under instruction from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will be even more aggressive about stopping the yen from rising than it was before.\par \par \uc2 Japanese trade and Japanese deflation.\par \par \uc2 Last week, new data showed that the country had run a trade deficit in 2011 for the first time since 1981, partly because of the impact the yen's recent strength has had on Japanese exports.\par \par \uc2 The authorities will be more keen than ever to prevent a strong yen from incurring more damage to the country's export prospects, especially the weak state of Japanese domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 But probably of even more concern for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will be the risk of more deflation. As \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  points out, a stronger yen will push import prices even lower and, with nominal interest rates down as far as they can go, the real level of rates will start to rise.\par \par \uc2 "Nothing would be more dangerous for the fiscal situation of Japan," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 So as the dollar falls back down close to its recent low at Y75.31, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will be coming under pressure to stop the move and stop it soon.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on twitter @NickHastingsDJ) [ 30-01-12 1130GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120130e81u000wf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
