\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 2,094 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2012\par 00:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    76.86-77.08      76.65-77.34 \par EUR/USD   1.2975-1.3053    1.2930-1.3077 \par AUD/USD   1.0453-1.0574    1.0379-1.0752 \par NZD/USD   0.8040-0.8142    0.7990-0.8241 \par GBP/USD   1.5515-1.5602    1.5448-1.5629 \par USD/CHF   0.9240-0.9333    0.9174-0.9388 \par USD/CAD   1.0048-1.0120    1.0000-1.0162 \par EUR/JPY    99.50-100.49     98.90-100.68 \par EUR/GBP   0.8311-0.8378    0.8274-0.8410 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to range-trade; liquidity thin in Asia as markets in several East Asian countries remain closed for Lunar New Year holidays. Spotlight on BOJ monetary policy meeting decision: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to keep its interest rate target in existing 0.0%-0.1% range, won't take any new policy action but cut its growth forecasts from 3 months ago. No strong cue for yen-funded carry trades from Wall Street overnight as U.S. stocks closed narrowly mixed (DJIA down 0.09%, S&P up 0.05%), though VIX fear gauge rose 2.13% to 18.67. USD/JPY supported by JPY sales on rebounding EUR/JPY cross amid speculation EU policy makers would make progress this week in taming euro-zone debt crisis; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand; expectations of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF. But USD/JPY topside limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter sales; caution ahead of tomorrow's U.S. FOMC interest rate decision. Data focus: 0600 GMT Japan November revised machine tool orders, 1500 GMT U.S. January Richmond Fed business activity survey. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode but stochastics turning bearish, while 5- & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Resistance at 77.08 (yesterday's high), then at 77.31-77.34 (Friday's high-Jan. 6 high, near 55-day moving average); breach would expose upside to 77.74 (Dec. 30 high), then 78.04 (Dec. 28 high), 78.23-78.29 band (Dec. 23 high-Nov. 29 high) and 78.41 (Nov. 2 high). Support at 76.86 (yesterday's low), then at 76.69-76.65 band (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low) and 76.55 (Jan. 17 minor reaction low); breach would expose downside to 76.30 (2-month low when hit Jan. 2, matching 76.4% Fibonacci correction of Oct. 31's 75.31-79.55 spike), then 75.31 (Oct. 31 record low) and psychological 75.00 level.\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting near-3-week high of 1.3053 yesterday. Supported by hopes for potential resolution in Greece as country negotiates a debt-restructuring agreement with its private creditors; news that Germany is ready to boost combined firepower of euro-zone EFSF and ESM rescue funds to EUR750 billion in exchange for strict budget rules in new fiscal compact for all euro-zone members; surprise rise in euro-zone consumer confidence indicator to minus 20.6 this month from minus 21.3 in December (vs forecast for drop to minus 21.5); robust French sales of EUR8.202 billion in 13-, 24-, and 50-week Treasury bills with yields close to where they were in previous auctions despite recent loss of its triple-A rating by S&P; sovereign demand for euro. But EUR/USD gains tempered by Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying yesterday Spain's economy will likely contract around 1.5% this year, making it even harder for the country to reach ambitious targets in cutting its budget deficit; worries over wider European economic slowdown; concerns about fiscal health of Portugal as 10-year Portuguese yield stays elevated above 14%. Data focus: 0830 GMT German January flash PMI, 0900 GMT euro-zone January flash PMI, 1000 GMT euro-zone November new industrial orders. EUR/USD daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday; 5-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising. Resistance at 1.3053 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.3077 (Jan. 3 reaction high), then 1.3095 (Dec. 23 high), 1.3120 (Dec. 22 high) and 1.3199 (Dec. 21 high). Support at 1.2975 (hourly chart), then at 1.2930 (hourly chart) and 1.2854 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 1.2839 (Thursday's low), then 1.2732 (Wednesday's low), 1.2648 (Jan. 17 low), 1.2624 (Jan. 13 16-month low, matching Aug. 31, 2010 low) and 1.2588 (Aug. 24, 2010 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting near-3-month high of 1.0574 yesterday. Supported by buoyant commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 3.67 yesterday at 313.58); diminished worries over euro-zone debt crisis; AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by expectations of 25-bp rate cut by RBA next month. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 1.0574 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0752 (Oct. 27 reaction high), then 1.0764 (Sept. 1 reaction high). Support at 1.0453 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.0379 (Friday's low), then 1.0366 (Thursday's low), 1.0354 (Wednesday's low), 1.0298 (Jan. 17 low) and 1.0248 (Jan. 