\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2012\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER SAYS US COOPERATION NEEDED FOR STRONGER IMF ROLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Friday that U.S. cooperation is needed if the International Monetary Fund is going to help tackle financial crises such as the one in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN BANKS MAY DRAW EUR400B ECB FUNDING FEB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European banks could next month take up to EUR400 billion worth of added funding from the European Central Banks' emergency borrowing facility, which could then lead to a reopening of funding markets, analysts from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALIAN BANKS SINGLE LARGEST USERS OF ECB 3-YEAR FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian banks were the single largest users of the special three-year long-term refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or LTRO, launched by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in December, according to a research report released by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  which shows how much European banks' dependence on ECB lines has risen significantly in recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: GREECE, CREDITORS CITE PROGRESS ON A DEBT PACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Greek government and its private-sector creditors appeared to be closing in on a debt-restructuring deal on the basis of new proposals, raising hopes it would pave the way for another multibillion-euro bailout for the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRELIMINARY CHINA JANUARY PMI UP SLIGHTLY, STILL UNDER 50 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An initial gauge showed that manufacturing activity in China contracted for the third straight month in January, the latest sign that growth in the world's second-largest economy is still slowing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU FISCAL DRAFT: NO ESM ASSISTANCE FOR COUNTRIES WHO DON'T APPROVE PACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone member states who do not ratify last month's fiscal pact won't be able to receive assistance from the region's permanent bailout mechanism, the latest draft of the agreement says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BUNDESBANK DOMBRET: CLARITY NEEDED ON FINANCIAL-TRANSACTION TAX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A board member of the German Bundesbank on Friday called for a consistent global implementation of the planned and much-debated financial-transaction tax to prevent competitiveness problems from arising.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU BARNIER SAYS BLOC WILL EMERGE STRONGER FROM CRISIS, QUESTIONS RATINGS AGENCIES' TRANSPARENCY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European governments have the political conviction needed to take the necessary tough anticrisis measures, European Financial Markets Commissioner Michel Barnier said Friday in a speech in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE BROADBENT: MPC NOT PRE-COMMITTED TO MORE EASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England rate-setters aren't pre-committed to further asset purchases in February, BOE policy maker Ben Broadbent was quoted as saying Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN DEC PPI -0.4% MM, +4.0% YY; LOWER THAN EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German factory prices in December fell on the month and grew far more slowly on the year than expected, data showed Friday, confirming expectations that consumer price inflation is set to fall in the course of this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK RETAIL SALES RISE IN DECEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. retail sales volume rose in December, despite the slowing economy, boosted by record sales of clothing and footwear and mild winter weather, official data showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S PM SAYS RBA HAS ROOM TO EASE POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Prime Minister Julia Gillard reiterated Friday her pledge to return the budget to surplus and said the country has room to ease official interest rates further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA 2011 FISCAL REVENUE SURGES 24.8%; DEFICIT NARROWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's fiscal deficit narrowed substantially in 2011 on sharp revenue growth, giving more weight to arguments that the government should boost spending to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA BANKS SEEN ISSUING UP TO CNY1 TRILLION NEW YUAN LOANS THIS MONTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banks will likely issue around CNY900 billion to CNY1 trillion in new yuan loans in January, the state-run China Securities Journal reported Friday, citing unnamed people in the industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA BANK CHIEFS: TO SECURE MORE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIQUIDITY IF CONDITIONS ALLOW - BOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's leading banks plan to borrow more from overseas to prepare for a potential worsening of external conditions, although they have already secured enough foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity needed for the first half of the year, the Bank of Korea said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PROSPECTS DIM FOR TAIWAN EXPORT ORDERS, FACTORY OUTPUT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for Taiwan's export orders and industrial output is dim, at least for the first half, the government said Friday, after the island posted weaker-than-expected data for December as demand from Europe and China continued to deteriorate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par INTERVIEW: EU BARNIER SAYS EURO NOT IN CRISIS, BUT ECONOMIES FACE DIFFICULITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union's financial markets commissioner said Friday he doesn't think the future of the euro is in jeopardy, but rather the central problem is with the region's economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW: EX-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF SAKAKIBARA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHOULDN'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN EURO/YEN MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's worsening economic picture suggests that the euro could fall further against the yen in the coming months, possibly as much as to near Y90, but Japan's government shouldn't try to fight the euro weakness through market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the trend is too strong for such action to have any meaningful impact, a former top Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 20-01-12 1004GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120120e81k000kt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
