\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2012\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRELIMINARY CHINA JANUARY PMI UP SLIGHTLY, STILL UNDER 50 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An initial gauge showed that manufacturing activity in China contracted for the third straight month in January, the latest sign that growth in the world's second-largest economy is still slowing. (published 0335 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: EU BARNIER SAYS EURO NOT IN CRISIS, BUT ECONOMIES FACE DIFFICULITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union's financial markets commissioner said Friday he doesn't think the future of the euro is in jeopardy, but rather the central problem is with the region's economies. (published 0610 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: EU BARNIER SAYS BLOC WILL EMERGE STRONGER FROM CRISIS, QUESTIONS RATINGS AGENCIES' TRANSPARENCY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European governments have the political conviction needed to take the necessary tough anticrisis measures, European Financial Markets Commissioner Michel Barnier said Friday in a speech in Tokyo. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER SAYS US COOPERATION NEEDED FOR STRONGER IMF ROLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Friday that U.S. cooperation is needed if the International Monetary Fund is going to help tackle financial crises such as the one in the euro zone. (published 0435 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: EX-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF SAKAKIBARA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SHOULDN'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN EURO/YEN MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's worsening economic picture suggests that the euro could fall further against the yen in the coming months, possibly as much as to near Y90, but Japan's government shouldn't try to fight the euro weakness through market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the trend is too strong for such action to have any meaningful impact, a former top Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Friday. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA 2011 FISCAL REVENUE SURGES 24.8%; DEFICIT NARROWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's fiscal deficit narrowed substantially in 2011 on sharp revenue growth, giving more weight to arguments that the government should boost spending to support the economy. (published 0243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S PM SAYS RBA HAS ROOM TO EASE POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Prime Minister Julia Gillard reiterated Friday her pledge to return the budget to surplus and said the country has room to ease official interest rates further. (published 0013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA BANKS SEEN ISSUING UP TO CNY1 TRILLION NEW YUAN LOANS THIS MONTH -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banks will likely issue around CNY900 billion to CNY1 trillion in new yuan loans in January, the state-run China Securities Journal reported Friday, citing unnamed people in the industry. (published 0006 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA URBAN UNEMPLOYMENT RATE 4.1% IN 2011 - XINHUA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's urban unemployment rate stood at 4.1% in 2011, the same as a year earlier, the official Xinhua news agency Friday reported the Ministry of Human Resources and Social Security as saying. (published 0249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: GUANGZHOU MULLS FINE-TUNING HOME PURCHASE LIMITS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Guangzhou city government is considering fine-tuning an existing ban on additional home purchases, including the possible introduction of different types of purchase limits for different types of homes, China Business News reported Friday, citing a housing official. (published 0640 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA BANK CHIEFS: TO SECURE MORE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIQUIDITY IF CONDITIONS ALLOW - BOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's leading banks plan to borrow more from overseas to prepare for a potential worsening of external conditions, although they have already secured enough foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity needed for the first half of the year, the Bank of Korea said Friday. (published 0636 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM TRADE DEFICIT $269 MLN IN DECEMBER VS $567 MLN IN NOVEMBER - CUSTOMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's trade deficit narrowed to $269 million in December from $567 million in November, customs figures showed Friday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM HANOI JANUARY PRICES LIKELY UP 0.96% ON MONTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Hanoi are likely to have risen 0.96% in January from a month earlier, accelerating from December's 0.61% rise, local media reported Friday, citing the capital's Department of Statistics. (published 0508 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THAI AIRWAYS\f28 \uc2 \f2  PLANS TO SELL THB1B 7-YEAR BOND, THB2B 10-YEAR BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai Airways International PCL\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to sell THB1 billion of seven-year bonds and THB2 billion of 10-year bonds via a private placement, Chairman Ampon Kittiampon said Friday. (published 0652 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: S KOREA GROWTH LIKELY SLOWED IN 4Q; TO EASE FURTHER IN 1Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economic growth likely slowed in the final quarter of last year from the previous quarter and is expected to weaken further in the current quarter, adding to the pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease monetary policy, economists said. (published 0237 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: BANK OF THAILAND LIKELY TO CUT RATE ON HEIGHTENED GROWTH RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to make its second consecutive policy interest rate cut when its policy committee meets Wednesday because of increased risk to economic growth, especially that posed by the euro zone sovereign debt problem. (published 0528 GMT) [ 20-01-12 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120120e81k000ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
