\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Azumi Signals Slim Chance Of Imminent Action To Boost Euro\b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2012\par 05:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's finance minister reiterates concerns over the impact the euro's weakness is having on Japanese exporters\par \par \uc2 -- Finance Minister Jun Azumi says he wants to carefully examine euro-yen falls\par \par \uc2 -- Azumi says there is no change in Japan's policy of buying EFSF bonds, despite their recent credit-rating downgrade\par \par \uc2 (Adds finance minister's comments in 4th, 10th paragraphs, background throughout)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Tuesday reiterated his concerns about the euro's weakness versus the yen, but added he still wants to "carefully" examine the exchange rate, suggesting Tokyo isn't likely to act anytime soon to support the embattled European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet in a sign of frustration with Europe's debt crisis--seen as the major cause of the euro's weakness--Finance Minister Jun Azumi continued to urge European leaders to quickly build credible firewalls to contain the turmoil. He indicated that Japan, for its part, will lend more money to Europe's bailout fund, the European Financial Stability Facility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro hit a 11-year low of Y97.04 Monday after ratings agency Standard and Poor's cut last week cut the sovereign ratings of nine European countries. At around 0407 GMT Tuesday, the euro was changing hands at Y97.68.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sliding euro is "having a major impact" on Japanese firms exporting to Europe, Azumi said at a regular news conference. "There is a possibility that this is starting to affect the overall volume of production in regions that produce (Europe-bound exports)."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Falls in the euro-yen rate make Japanese products less competitive in the euro-linked market. This, in turn, harms the export-led economy whose rebound from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami has largely run out of steam. European Union nations buy roughly 10% of Japan's exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'd likely to carefully examine current movement" in the exchange rate, Azumi said. "I expect discussions among (European) countries will accelerate. I'd like to deal with this issue carefully and cautiously while watching (the debates), as exchange rates sometimes make sharp movements."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi's caution is in line with the market view that Japan would be wary of taking steps to lift the euro versus the yen in the face of objections from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since last August to strengthen the dollar against the yen have been criticized by the ECB, which appeared worried that such action could set a precedent for developing economies such as China, which have kept their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak to boost exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi played down S&P's move Monday to downgrade the long-term credit rating on the EFSF to AA+ from AAA.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think this will immediately shake the credibility of EFSF bonds," which Japan has bought regularly since the fund's inaugural debt auction last January, Azumi said. "We have bought them as important products for stable management (of Japan's foreign reserves), something that is worth holding, and there won't be any immediate change in our thinking."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, \f28 \uc2 \f2 