\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2012\par 10:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA INFLATION SLOWS IN DECEMBER; POINTS TO EASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in China slowed slightly in December, but was marginally above market expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN FINAL DEC CPI +0.7% ON MONTH; +2.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Germany rose 0.7% on the month during December and 2.1% from a year earlier, data from the federal statistics office, Destatis, confirmed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN FIN MIN PRAISES ITALIAN DEBT MOVES, OPPOSES EURO BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy has found a good balance between debt reduction and structural reforms to promote economic growth, German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said at a closed-door conference Wednesday, the Die Welt newspaper reported Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOODY'S KEEPS SWEDEN'S AAA RATING AMID SOUND PUBLIC FINANCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit rating agency Moody's Investors Service Thursday said it maintains the highest possible rating for Swedish government debt, citing the country's resilient economy and low public debt level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRANCE'S NOV CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT NARROWED TO EUR2.3B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's current account deficit narrowed to EUR2.3 billion in November from EUR4.2 billion in October as a result of a sharp reduction in the goods deficit, the Bank of France said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH JAN-NOV BUDGET DEFICIT NARROWS TO EUR97.2B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's budget gap narrowed in the first 11 months of the year, as expenditure showed a marked fall, suggesting the French government is advancing in its plan to bring the country's budget deficit under control.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN NOV CURRENT ACCT SURPLUS Y138.5B, BELOW EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a smaller-than-expected surplus of Y138.5 billion in its current account for November as exporters were hit by a strong yen, supply problems due to floods in Thailand, and Europe's economic turmoil, official data showed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN DEC 1-20 TRADE DEFICIT Y496.5 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a merchandise trade deficit of Y496.5 billion in the first 20 days of December, compared with a Y33.96 billion surplus in the year-earlier period, the finance ministry said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ CHIEF ECONOMIST: JAPAN GROWTH TO STAGNATE, BUT GRADUAL RECOVERY WILL FOLLOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy is likely to remain stagnant for the time being before returning to a gradual recovery path on the back of post-quake reconstruction demand and a pickup in growth in emerging economies, the Bank of Japan's chief economist said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN WARNS OF GLOBAL IMPACT FROM U.S. VOLCKER RULE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government warned of broad potential consequences for its banks, the JGB market and sovereign debt markets globally from the U.S. "Volcker Rule" that is meant to limit proprietary trading in some types of speculative instruments by large banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EX-BOJ OFFICIAL IWATA: EURO COULD FALL TO ALL-TIME LOW BELOW ALL-TIME LOW OF AROUND Y88.90 THIS YR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Kazumasa Iwata said Thursday that there is a risk of the euro falling to an all-time low against the yen this year, pointing to the continued uncertainty over the sovereign debt crisis in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA FACTORY OUTPUT REBOUNDS IN NOV, MAY WEIGH ON RATE DECISION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's industrial output rose in November, bouncing back from a dismal reading the previous month and lessening the urgency for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to begin loosening monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : SOLD US DOLLARS WORTH $2.9 BLN IN NOVEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday said that in November it sold U.S. dollars worth $2.9 billion, its largest monthly sale of the greenback in nearly two years, indicating that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to support the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TELLS GEITHNER IT WILL REDUCE IRANIAN CRUDE IMPORTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister told visiting U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner Thursday that his country will take steps to reduce its dependency on oil imports from Iran, taking a much more conciliatory approach to the matter than China did a few days ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: CHINA WON'T STOP IMPORTING IRANIAN CRUDE OIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China won't stop importing Iranian crude oil and has no reason to blindly adhere to a U.S. sanctions policy, a researcher at a Chinese think-tank wrote in a front-page commentary published in the People's Daily Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA OFFICIAL: HAVEN'T TOLD REFINERS TO CUT CRUDE IMPORTS FROM IRAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's government hasn't told refiners to reduce imports of crude oil from Iran because of tightening sanctions by the U.S. and Europe, a senior oil ministry official said Thursday. [ 12-01-12 0901GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120112e81c000ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
