\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan Wants To Keep Importing Iranian Crude Oil -MOFA Official\b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2012\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 10 Jan 2012 21:22 EDT *DJ  Japan Worried About Energy Supply Disruptions -MOFA Official\par \par \uc2 10 Jan 2012 21:25 EDT DJ  Japan Wants To Keep Importing Iranian Crude Oil -MOFA Official\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Japan wants to keep importing crude oil from Iran despite rising pressure from the U.S., its key ally, to cooperate in strengthening sanctions over uranium enrichment.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned about an impact on global energy prices," an official at Japan's Ministry of Foreign Affairs said Wednesday. "We are also worried about how a fuel shortage might impact rebuilding" after the massive earthquake and tsunami that hit Japan in March, added the official, who asked not to be named.\par \par \uc2 The comments came a day before the planned meeting between U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner and Japan's Finance Minister Jun Azumi. The two ministers plan to meet Thursday morning, with a possible ban on Iranian crude oil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to be on the agenda, which a spokesman at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to discuss.\par \par \uc2 -By Mari Iwata, \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\uc2 ; 813-6269-2798; mari.iwata@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 10, 2012 21:25 ET (02:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120111e81b000y4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
