\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: US To Press China, Japan On Iran Steps\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sudeep Reddy  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,041 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2012\par 21:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner headed for China and Japan on Sunday to seek support for boosting the financial pressure on Iran in an effort to prevent Tehran from developing nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 But winning their help could be complicated, coming shortly after the U.S. publicly chastised both countries for their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and amid escalating trade tensions with Beijing.\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration is attempting to squeeze Iran's government by curtailing its oil revenue. The European Union is tacking the same tack, agreeing in principle last week to enact an embargo on all purchases of Iranian oil. A new U.S. law imposes U.S. sanctions on Iran's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and could penalize foreign firms that trade with the bank, which handles Iran's oil revenue.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Investors are watching nervously, because any retaliation by Iran could send oil prices skyrocketing and threaten a fragile global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury official said of the oil embargo, "We believe that a well-timed, phased reduction can cut Iranian revenues without disrupting international crude oil markets."\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary Leon Panetta, speaking Sunday on CBS's "Face the Nation," said, "Our red line to Iran is to not develop a nuclear weapon...the responsible thing to do right now is to keep putting diplomatic and economic pressure on them to force them to do the right thing."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Panetta, responding to a threat published Sunday in Iranian newspapers by a senior Iranian military commander that Tehran "won't allow a drop of oil to pass through the Strait of Hormuz" if the country's oil exports are blocked, according to the Associated Press, said, "We made very clear that the United States will not tolerate the blocking of the Straits of Hormuz."\par \par \uc2 The remarks came as Iran has begun uranium enrichment at a new underground site well protected from possible airstrikes, a leading hardline newspaper reported Sunday, the AP reported. They also came as Iran President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad arrived in Venezuela for the start of a four-day tour of Latin America, seeking some moral support at a time when Iran faces growing international isolation over its nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Geithner, who left Sunday for Beijing, faces strong resistance from China, the biggest export market for Iranian crude. Though China has curtailed its Iranian oil imports over a pricing dispute, it has declined to join the embargo.\par \par \uc2 His agenda in Beijing will include other concerns generating friction between the two nations, including trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China last month slapped new tariffs on some U.S. vehicles, while the Obama administration is pressing China on its trade practices on chickens and solar panels.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has long maintained that China is keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, artificially undervalued to juice its exports. But the Treasury, in a report last month, declined to label China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, despite harsh words over the country's practices.\par \par \uc2 Designating China as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" would be a significant ratcheting up of public pressure on Beijing, but in practical terms would do little besides trigger new talks between the U.S. and China on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the Obama administration faces pressure from Congress to do it, analysts say the label would accomplish little if China isn't willing to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 "The administration is damned if it does and damned if it doesn't," said Edwin Truman, a senior fellow at the Peterson Institute for International Economics and former senior Treasury official. "Designating China at this point only makes you feel good and probably is counterproductive."\par \par \uc2 New signs of a global economic slowdown are limiting any prospect of faster yuan appreciation. Meanwhile, with China preparing to start a leadership change this fall, Beijing has little incentive to yield to U.S. economic concerns.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury official said Mr. Geithner would press China on Washington's continuing economic concerns. "Ensuring that American businesses and workers are competing on a level playing field with China is a top priority of this administration," the official said. "While we've seen some progress, there's still a long way to go and we will keep fighting for these priorities and delivering that message at every chance."\par \par \uc2 In Beijing, Mr. Geithner will meet Tuesday and Wednesday with Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao, Vice Premier Wang Qishan, Vice President Xi Jinping and Executive Vice Premier Li Keqiang. On Thursday in Tokyo, he will meet with Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda, Finance Minister Jun Azumi and other senior government officials.\par \par \uc2 The meetings are expected to include a discussion of Europe's debt troubles. The U.S., Japan and China all believe euro-zone nations haven't done enough to stem the crisis.\par \par \uc2 The talks in Japan will be the first high-level meeting since the Treasury, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report last month, chided \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for two unilateral market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year to lower the yen's value. In unusually tough public words for its ally, Treasury said Japan "should take fundamental and thoroughgoing steps to increase the dynamism of the domestic economy, increase the competitiveness of Japanese firms...and raise potential growth."\par \par \uc2 While Japanese officials gave no indication they would adjust their policies in response, the report reflected the Obama administration's concerns about measures by other countries that could hurt U.S. exports.\par \par \uc2 As the euro-zone turmoil worsens, investors are expected to seek haven in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the dollar and the yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could limit the yen's appreciation temporarily, helping its exports, at the same time the dollar could rise, hurting U.S. exports.\par \par \uc2 "With the euro where it is, with capital not flowing that much to emerging markets, it's all coming back home to the U.S.," said Eswar Prasad, a Brookings Institution economist and professor of trade policy at Cornell University. "Right now, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with enough heft to share some of the burden of adjustment."\par \par \uc2 --Louise Radnofsky contributed to this article. [ 01-08-12 1509ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120108e81800010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
