\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares May Nudge Lower; Eurozone Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2012\par 06:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen lower, bunds higher; euro, oil lower; gold higher\f28 \par \par \uc2 -German Econ Min: Euro Has No Alternative For Germany-Report\par \par \uc2 -Bundesbank chief says Germany must not waver over deficit\par \par \uc2 -Libya Says No Review Of ENI Oil, Gas Deals\par \par \uc2 -Iran Says Ready For Nuclear Talks As Tensions Mount\par \par \uc2 -Iran Is Refusing To Fuel Some European, Arab Airlines -AFP\par \par \uc2 -Iran Oil Minister Says Crude Will Exceed $200 A Barrel If Iran Oil Sanctioned - Agency\par \par \uc2 -Olympics, Diamond Jubilee Will Boost UK In 2012 -- Cameron\par \par \uc2 -Iran Produces Nuclear Rod\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0855 GER Dec Manufacturing PMI; 0900 EU Dec Eurozone Manufacturing PMI; US New Year's Day Holiday Observed; Markets, banks, businesses and govt offices closed.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean stock market lost hold of early gains Monday, trading lower with most other major bourses closed for the New Year holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing unexpectedly rebounded in December on holiday shopping, official data showed Sunday, as the world's number two economy showed some resilience despite strife in key export markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The purchasing managers index (PMI) reached 50.3 in December, the China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European investors may take a cautious approach Monday, with stock markets starting a bit lower and bunds nudging higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the U.K. closed, other European stock markets may open lower on a circumspect note Monday, as investors enter the New Year with the ongoing eurozone's problems in mind.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The destiny of 2012 lies in the hands of the EU politicians," says David Buik at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BGC Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding that the crisis has really rattled sentiment. "However, company P/E ratios are at their cheapest since 1995 and offer real long-term value, provided the banking industry does not implode," adds Buik.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The beginning of the year always brings the potential for a "January rally", but Martin Munk at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jyske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said he would be cautious. He noted that with the current tension in the market, this is an abnormal January. "However, I can't rule out a positive opening," Munk said. "There may be new money in the market, and investors looking at their portfolios with fresh eyes."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His main advice to investors in the current situation is to be cautious and maintain a wide stock portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jyske's main scenario for the beginning of the year is for markets to remain volatile, and for the pricing of cyclical stocks to stay low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The one bright spark to 2011 has been the resilience of the United States, given the weak global economic backdrop," said dealer Jonathan Sudaria at trading group Capital Spreads. .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "However, with the European debt crisis still outpacing policymakers' ability to curtail it, and uncertainty over how China's slowdown will land, downside risks are already lining up for 2012."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.S. it came down to the wire, but in the final minute of 2011 trading, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  500-stock index closed the year virtually unchanged from where it closed in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 closed Friday down 5.42 points, or 0.43%, to 1257.60. The full-year decline of 0.04 of a point, or 0.0028%, was the smallest annual move since at least 1947, according to preliminary S&P calculations. S&P Indices scrambled after the close of trading to determine an extra decimal place in the 1947 data to figure out which was the slimmer move of the two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fared better in 2011. The Dow finished down 69.48 points, or 0.57%, to 12217.56 on Friday, but closed the year up 5.53%. The blue-chip index closed the fourth quarter up 12%, its biggest quarterly percentage jump since 2003.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite fell 8.59 points, or 0.3%, to 2605.15, and closed the year down 1.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The year was defined by fits and starts related to Europe's sovereign-debt crisis and global economic growth concerns, prompting investors to flock to defensive and dividend-paying stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My wish list for 2012 is for the money that continues to gravitate to the safety of the U.S. Treasury market to get a little more nerve and come back into equities," said Benedict Willis III, director of floor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for investment-banking boutique Sunrise Securities. "That will be important for equities to continue the recent upward move."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Munk said the turn of events in the eurozone could also lead to either a positive development, which should boost cyclicals and financials, or a more gloomy outcome with another major downturn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That's the two outside scenarios, but the main scenario is a bit like this. Volatility still ahead," Munk said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is lower Monday as Europe's debt woes continue into the New Year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday, the European single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slumped to 99.97 yen in London -- which was the lowest level since December 2000 and the first time it has breached the key 100-yen barrier since June 2001.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It later recovered to stand at 100.19 yen, still below 100.61 yen on Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Euro/yen is the ultimate risk-off trade at the moment," \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com research director Kathleen Brooks said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That is where investors' fears regarding the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are being manifested," adding that risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the yen "has just notched up a gear".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the euro held above $1.29, trading at $1.2958 in the afternoon. On Thursday it had tumbled to $1.2858 -- the lowest since September 14, 2010 -- before rebounding on brighter US economic data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds may start a bit higher Monday amid a Bundesbank warning that Germany must cut its deficit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German government must keep up its fight to reduce the public deficit despite the grim economic outlook, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Jens Weidmann said in an interview to be published Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The second and final release of the euro zone composite purchasing managers for December is expected to confirm the mild improvement reported in the preliminary survey. But, with the balance remaining below the key 50 level, concerns that the region's economy is already contracting will only increase.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.K., the government is determined to do more to help the economy recover, and use the London Olympic games and celebrations to mark the queen's 60th year on the throne to show what the country can achieve despite the trying times, Prime Minister David Cameron said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury bonds have handed investors a return of 9.66% through last Thursday on the back of 5.87% gain in 2010, according to data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Treasury yields are back at panic levels," said Michael Gayed, chief investment strategist at Pension Partners. "The bond market is sensing something very serious is coming in 2012, yet stock investors not so much. The answer of who is right, the bond market or the stock market, probably gets answered in the next three months and a trend will assert itself."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures settled lower Friday in the final trading session of the year as conflicting news from France and China gave divergent signals for global demand in 2012. Light, sweet crude for February delivery settled down 82 cents, or 0.8%, at $98.83 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent crude on the ICE Futures Europe exchange was down 63 cents, or 0.6%, at $107.38 a barrel. Volume was about half of normal levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "America is getting used to the idea that we have a price to pay for high oil," said Carl Larry, president of research firm Oil Outlooks & Opinions. "Many people are dismissing the idea of $100 oil. But this is the reality. It's going to be higher next year. We're going to see improvement in the economy. We've proven over the past year that the economy can grow with high oil prices. We grow; we adapt."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of copper and nickel rose on increased industrial demand and reports of a firming global trend. Comex gold was up $28.30 at $1,569.20.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-01-12 0556GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12217.56   -69.48   -0.57 % \par Nasdaq    2605.15    -8.59    -0.33 % \par S&P 500   1257.60    -5.42    -0.43 % \par FTSE 100  5572.28    +5.51    +0.10 % \par Xetra DAX 5898.35   +49.57    +0.85 % \par CAC40     3159.81    32.25     1.03% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   Closed \par Hang Seng    Closed \par S&P/ASX 200   Closed \par Taiwan Index  6955.63  -116.45  -1.65% \par S.Korea Kospi 1818.40    -7.34  -0.40% \par Shanghai Comp Closed \par Bombay SenSex 15454.88   -0.04   0.00% \par Dow Future    Closed \par NASDAQ Future Closed \par S&P Future    Closed \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par  \par USD/JPY      76.92-98         -0.01% \par Range        77.16    76.94 \par EUR/USD      1.2923-30        -0.11% \par Range        1.2952   1.2924 \par AUD/USD      1.0219-24        +0.08% \par Range        1.0231   1.0208 \par GBP/USD      1.5520-26         0.00% \par Range        1.5541   1.5500 \par USD/CHF      0.9374-420       +0.06% \par Range        0.9395   0.9386 \par EUR/GBP      0.8327-47       -1.08% \par Range        0.8338   0.8345 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2160-74        +0.16% \par Range        1.2167   1.2150 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 24/32      up 1/32 0.243%   dn 2.3 \par 5Y Tsy   100 7/32      up 6/32 0.832%   dn 4.1 \par 10Y Tsy  101 3/32      up 5/32 1.786%   dn 1.9 \par 10Y JGB Closed \par Closing Treasury prices vs prior NY close \par  \par Asian Spot Gold $1572.38 +$28.00 +1.8% \par Comex Gold      $1569.20 +$28.30 +2.0% \par Brent Crude Oil $107.38  -$0.63 -0.6% \par Above are as of 0500 GMT vs NY close \par  \par CALENDAR: \par Monday, January 2, 2012                               Exp        Prev \par GMT \par 0845  ITA  Dec     Manufacturing PMI \par                       PMI Manufacturing               43.8       44 \par 0850  FRA  Dec     Manufacturing PMI \par         