\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: US Criticizes Japan, China On  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley and Sudeep Reddy   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2011\par 03:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Obama administration again declined to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, while it criticized Japan's efforts to limit the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department, in its overdue semiannual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, offered its most direct criticism to date of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the U.S. didn't support. The move could complicate any further attempts by Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Treasury also said in the report, released Tuesday, that it would continue to press Beijing for greater exchange-rate flexibility, as well as to level the playing field for foreign companies and to shift China's economy away from export-led growth.\par \par \uc2 In the report, the department said that "While China's real exchange rate has appreciated, the process of appreciation remains incomplete." The report noted that the yuan has appreciated nearly 12% against the U.S. dollar on an inflation-adjusted basis since June 2010.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 "China's longstanding pattern of reserve accumulation, the persistence of its current-account surplus and the incomplete appreciation, especially given rapid productivity growth in the traded-goods sector, indicate that the real exchange rate of the renminbi is persistently misaligned and remains substantially undervalued," the report said.\par \par \uc2 The latest report, which will likely further inflame China critics in Congress, was released while lawmakers were away for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the Chinese Embassy in Washington weren't immediately available to comment.\par \par \uc2 On Japan, the Treasury report noted that "rather than reacting to domestic 'strong yen' concerns by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to influence the exchange rate, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should take fundamental and thoroughgoing steps to increase the dynamism of the domestic economy, increase the competitiveness of Japanese firms -- including those in utilities and services -- and raise potential growth."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and other Group of Seven nations joined \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the March 11 earthquake. But the U.S. declined to back Japan's two latest moves, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be based on market fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Market participants in Japan said early Wednesday that they were surprised by the strong language used in the Treasury report and predicted that it would make it more difficult for Tokyo to again enter the market to try to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "We knew that the U.S. has not welcomed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but this clear a message was quite a surprise," said Junya Tanase, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo weren't immediately available to comment on the U.S. report. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop what it termed excess volatility and has said the yen's strength near postwar highs against the dollar wasn't warranted by the fundamentals of the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 The U.S., as expected, opted not to name China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator though it called the yuan appreciation "insufficient." Designating China as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" would be a significant ratcheting-up of public pressure on Beijing, but in practical terms would do little besides trigger new talks between the U.S. and China on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In October, Treasury had said it would delay the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, in the hopes of using global summits as leverage to persuade China to let the yuan appreciate more quickly.\par \par \uc2 It is clear the Obama administration is aware that the pace of yuan appreciation will slow next year: Economists in China have widely forecast that will happen because of slowing export growth.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, China is expected to have a current-account surplus under the 4% mark that U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner tried -- and failed -- to make a standard for Group of 20 review at the G-20 summit in November 2010. But the administration's hands are tied politically, given the important role China is playing in the presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Substantively, the White House is likely to argue that Beijing should continue with a more rapid pace of appreciation because that will help China make the transition to domestically led growth. But the real question is whether the Chinese will plan for any appreciation in the yuan at all.\par \par \uc2 --Jeff Bater and Bob Davis contributed to this article. [ 12-27-11 2142ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111228e7cs0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
