\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Renews Pledge To Act Vs Yen Despite US Criticism\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2011\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japanese government officials say nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy unchanged\par \par \uc2 -- Officials say Japan's government will act if yen rises too sharply\par \par \uc2 -- Officials say aware of U.S. Treasury report criticizing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds comment from economy minister in 3rd paragraph)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Japan put on a brave face Wednesday after the U.S. Treasury issued a surprisingly strong reprimand against Tokyo's recent rounds of yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the hardening U.S. attitude could seriously undermine further forays into the market in the months ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is no change in our thinking regarding Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, and there is no change in our stance that we will respond to excessive rises in the yen," a senior Japanese government official told reporters. "We have informed (Japan's foreign counterparts, including the U.S.) of that point through various channels, and we will explain it whenever that becomes necessary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economy Minister Motohisa Furukawa echoed that sentiment, telling \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2  that Japan "will take appropriate steps if needed because excessive market moves are bad for economies and financial markets."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The officials made the remarks after the Treasury Department, in its semiannual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report, offered its most direct criticism to date of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's two latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which the U.S. didn't support. With the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stiffly against Japanese unilateral actions, the Treasury's stance suggests Japan is effectively isolated on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy front among the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G-7 nations joined \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the country suffered the March 11 earthquake. But the U.S. declined to back Japan's two follow-up moves on Aug. 4 and Oct. 31, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be based on market fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the Japanese officials' reassurances, there are signs that the Treasury report caught Tokyo completely off guard. Some Japanese policymakers had been voicing relief recently that the U.S. hadn't made any public criticism after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record Y9-trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  between Oct. 28 and Nov. 28.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sensing a shift in the U.S. policy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders already started selling the dollar versus the yen early Wednesday. They are betting that Tokyo will find it more difficult to artificially stem the yen's advance versus the dollar if its U.S. ally is against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury report comes at a bad time for the government of Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda, whose public support is falling amid public discontent over his policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda cannot afford to let the yen keep rising from the current near-record levels of Y77, as Japan's export-led economy is losing steam after a brief rebound from the March 11 earthquake. Gains in the yen make Japanese exports less competitive overseas and encourage Japanese makers to shift their production abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. criticism is not surprising, especially since Washington has a clearly stated goal of increasing U.S. exports, an ambition not helped by a weaker yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Treasury's public reprimand is still something of a shock to Japan. Tokyo has long been aware that the U.S. government was unlikely to publicly support yen-selling, either by coordinated action or verbally, unless circumstances became quite severe. Therefore it was counting on U.S. Treasury and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials remaining largely silent on Japan's actions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fact that (U.S. Treasury) Secretary Geithner hasn't criticized our action means approval, a silence of approval," one official told \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the weeks following Tokyo's solo yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back in September 2010, the first such action in more than six years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. also didn't buy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's argument that its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were aimed at calming disorderly markets and thus in line with G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  principles. The report said markets appeared to be "orderly" at times of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's two previous solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gaining "understanding" from foreign countries for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been one of the key diplomatic goals of the government. Since he was named as finance minister in early September, Jun Azumi has "explained" Japan's stance through various international meetings, including G-7 to G-20 gatherings, and numerous bilateral meetings with his counterparts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The apparent failure to persuade Washington underscores how difficult it is to forge a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy agreement at a time when overall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility isn't at a level threatening the entire world. Weakening the yen would make Japanese products more competitive and save the nation's economy, but could hurt their overseas rivals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 28-12-11 0759GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111228e7cs0009q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
