\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro nears 1-year low vs dollar, hits 10-year low vs yen\b0\par\par\uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2011\par 19:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds detail about EUR/JPY, comment)\par \par \uc2 * Euro/dollar hits lowest levels since January\par \par \uc2 * Short-term Italian debt sale supports; bigger test Thursday\par \par \uc2 * Euro hits 10-year low vs yen in thin trade\par \par \uc2 * U.S. Treasury report criticizes Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Luciana Lopez and Steven C. Johnson\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 28 (Reuters) - The euro neared a one-year low against the dollar and hit a 10-year trough against the yen on Wednesday after data showed euro zone banks were still hoarding cash, spurring fears of a growing liquidity crunch the day before an important Italian bond sale.\par \par \uc2 Year-end conditions kept volumes light, but traders said investors in the market were spooked by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showing euro zone banks deposited a record 452 billion euros ($585.18 billion) with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Signs that banks were hoarding cash came just days after the ECB provided banks almost half a trillion euros worth of three-year loans at cut-rate prices to encourage lending and ease strains caused by the euro zone's two-year-old sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "If European banks are still this concerned, it's not a good sign," said Karl Schamotta, senior markets strategist with \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions.\par \par \uc2 Banks typically park only excess cash in the ECB's low-interest-rate deposit accounts, often at a loss. That they were doing so after accessing cheap ECB loans was troubling, Schamotta said.\par \par \uc2 "That underlines the possibility that this liquidity crunch is getting worse and will continue into the new year," he said. "This could mean a prolonged period of slow euro zone growth."\par \par \uc2 The euro fell as low as $1.2910, the lowest level since Jan. 10, before clawing back to $1.2934, about 1 percent below its level late Tuesday in New York. The slide below $1.30 triggered automatic sell orders that sped up the slide, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro hit its lowest level since June 2001 at 100.70. Investors often flock to the dollar and yen in times of trouble. The dollar was up 0.1 percent at 77.90 yen.\par \par \uc2 Safe-haven demand also lifted the dollar 0.9 percent against the Swiss franc to 0.9424 franc, while sterling shed 1.4 percent to $1.5451. Yields on 10-year U.S. Treasuries fell back below the 2 percent mark, a sign of strong demand.\par \par \uc2 The yen's surge will not delight policymakers in Tokyo, as it makes Japanese exports more costly, weighing down stock prices and putting more pressure on the sluggish economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times this year to weaken the yen by selling it on the open market. Whether they will act again, though, is unclear, particularly after a semi-annual U.S. Treasury report criticized two bouts of solo yen selling in August and October.\par \par \uc2 ITALIAN AUCTION AHEAD\par \par \uc2 Earlier optimism after Italy halved the price it pays for six-month loans faded after the ECB report and as traders looked ahead to Thursday's more challenging Italian auction of three- and 10-year government debt.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been shunning longer-maturity Italian government bonds over the last few months on fear that slow growth and a tough package of spending cuts and tax hikes will make it difficult for the country to finance its large public debt burden.\par \par \uc2 Italy must attract strong demand on Thursday from global investors to hold benchmark 10-year borrowing costs below the 7 percent level; markets fear that costs above 7 percent are not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 Even then, traders said one or two decent auctions will not solve Europe's two-year-old debt crisis, which has already forced Greece, Ireland and Portugal to seek emergency rescues.\par \par \uc2 "You can't make your decision based on one auction," said Ihab Salib, senior portfolio manager and head of international fixed-income at Federated Investors in Pittsburgh. "It's going to be an evolution of more than one event."\par \par \uc2 Traders emphasized that year-end liquidity was thin, exaggerating moves, but most said there was little reason to get long the euro before the year ends.\par \par \uc2 "It might pop up to $1.32 because of year-end squaring, but I haven't really seen it. There's no reason to own the euro going into the new year," said John Doyle, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Tempus Consulting in Washington.\par \par \uc2 While the euro is off about 3.2 percent against the dollar this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has swung widely as investors fret the 17-nation monetary union could face drastic changes because of the debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 That volatility could continue into next year, Salib said.\par \par \uc2 "Unless you're really doing your homework and you're capable of being fairly active, you're better off just staying away," he said. "Moves that used to take weeks and months to develop can happen in a 2- or 3-day period." ($1 = 0.7724 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 8)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111228e7cs00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
