\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-Japan Nov exports fall 4.5 pct yr/yr -MOF\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2011\par 01:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 21 (Reuters) - Japan's exports fell in November from a year earlier, marking the fastest decline in six months as a persistently strong yen, Europe's sovereign debt turmoil and a slowdown in emerging economies weighed on overseas \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand.   \par **************************************************************  \tab \par  KEY POINTS:  \tab \par  -- Exports dropped 4.5 percent in November from a year\par earlier, against a median forecast for a 4.0 percent annual\par decline, finance ministry data showed on Wednesday. \par \tab \par  -- Imports were up 11.4 percent in November from a year\par earlier, versus an 8.7 percent annual gain forecast by\par economists. \tab \par  -- The trade balance came to a 684.7 billion yen deficit,\par its second consecutive month of deficit. The median forecast was\par for a 450.0 billion yen deficit.     \tab \par  -- Exports to Asia, which account for more than half of\par Japan's total exports, declined 8.0 percent from a year earlier.\par Exports to the United States were up 2.0 percent from the same\par period a year ago.\tab \par    \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMENTARY:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YOSHIMASA MARUYAMA, CHIEF ECONOMIST, ITOCHU ECONOMIC RESEARCH INSTITUTE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The global economic slowdown and a strong yen weighed on exports, while Thailand's floods affected shipments as well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The adverse impact from Thailand's floods likely peaked in November but other factors such as faltering global growth, Europe's debt problems and a strong yen will likely continue to weigh on exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan's exports are expected to fall and its trade balance is likely to stay in deficit until the January-March quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a chance that the Bank of Japan will implement additional easing in January-March. Given the latest tankan corporation sentiment survey, it wouldn't be surprising if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased policy but it probably wants to save easing tools at the moment for possible further downside risks to the economy"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NAOKI IIZUKA, SENIOR ECONOMIST, MIZUHO SECURITIES, TOKYO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The adverse effect of the slowdown in Europe through a drop in Europe-bound exports and possible spillover onto demand in Asia has begun to emerge. In addition, there is the impact from prolonged inventory adjustment in Asia and from the Thai floods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe is likely to enter a serious recession so the outlook for exports to the region is dim but Asia-bound exports are likely to recover soon as China and other countries are likely to implement stimulus steps.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There will likely be no change in the BOJ's forecast of a return to a moderate recovery. The latest tankan suggests that business sentiment has not deteriorated enough to warrant additional easing at Wednesday's policy review."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HIDEKI MATSUMURA, SENIOR ECONOMIST, JAPAN RESEARCH INSTITUTE, TOKYO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The increase in imports is largely in line with expectations given a rise in crude oil and LNG purchases. What worries me is the larger-than-expected fall in exports, which indicates faster-than-anticipated slowdown in overseas economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This trend of slowing exports will be here to stay for the time being as demand for Japan's mainstay export products, such as machinery and components, is likely to remain sluggish."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   MARKET REACTION:  \tab \par  -- For yen updates click, for prices click  \tab \par  -- For JGB updates click, for prices click \par \tab \par  -- For stocks click, for Nikkei average click  \par \tab \par               \tab \par  LINKS:            \tab \par  -- Tables:  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.customs.go.jp/toukei/info/index_e.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.customs.go.jp/toukei/info/index_e.htm}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \tab \par  \tab \par  BACKGROUND:\tab \par  -- Japan's economy has rebounded from a recession triggered\par by the March earthquake and tsunami, but is expected to slow\par sharply this quarter as the boost from the restoration of supply\par chains and production facilities tails off.\tab \par  -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and eased\par monetary policy in October to reduce the pain from sharp yen\par rises and slowing overseas growth.\tab \par  -- The Bank of Japan is likely to keep its monetary policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 unchanged at a two-day meeting ending Wednesday, but worsening business sentiment, weak capital expenditure and Europe's sovereign debt crisis are challenging the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's view that the world's third-largest economy will recover early next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Stanley White, Kiyoshi Takenaka, Rie Ishiguro; Editing by Joseph Radford)\f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/TRADE (INSTANT VIEW 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111221e7cl0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
