\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Joint Measure May Soon Push Dollar Lower Against Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2011\par 09:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Central banks' coordinated dollar-pumping step decreased demand for greenback\par \par \uc2 -- Dollar may fall below Y70 by end of January, dealer says\par \par \uc2 -- Strategists say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat rises if dollar falls below Y76\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--The recent decision by the world's six leading central banks to lower the interest rate on dollar loans in order to ease market stresses may have the unanticipated impact of helping the yen resume its rise, traders say, posing fresh problems for Japan's policy makers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banks of the U.S., U.K., Canada, Japan, Europe and Switzerland on Nov. 30 cut rates at which they provide dollars to the market by 0.5 percentage point.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the move eased market worries over dollar liquidity, traders say it will also have a dollar-negative impact similar to the pressure on the greenback following the Fed's quantitative easing measures. The Fed's moves to buy Treasury bonds first announced in November 2010 has been seen as one of the main reasons the yen has remained so strong over the past 12 months, since it increased the supply of dollars in the market, effectively pushing down the greenback's value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen has reached a series of post-World War II record highs over the past nine months as investors look for a refuge from global financial turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The more dollars that banks borrow from the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) window, the more powerful the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's effect in pushing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower," said Hideki Amikura, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amikura said the effects, coupled with pessimism toward the U.S. economy and European debt problems, will likely send the greenback below Y70.00 by the end of January, well below the record low of Y75.31 marked on Oct. 31. As of 0745 GMT, the greenback was at Y77.96.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such sharp moves trigger another around of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , especially if the dollar falls below the Y76 mark, to protect the country's export-reliant economy, said Citibank Japan's chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Osamu Takashima.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, Economy Minister Motohisa Furukawa said in the recent interview with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2  that the government still believes are excessively strong, representing a major threat to Japan's . He underscored that Tokyo will keep making full-fledged efforts to fight the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  given that it has only around Y40 trillion left in the fund it uses to finance \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and it remains unclear whether other G-7 nations will give Japan a green light for further yen selling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since Japan's massive Y9 trillion yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around the end of October, the greenback has been locked in a narrow range around Y77. Traders say that's because investors, especially those in Europe, have hastily sought dollars for year-end funding needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, the cost to borrow the dollar against the yen on the forward market jumped as high as 1.7% before the central banks' action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At that point a lot of investors rushed to get dollars, and we saw unusual trading patterns," said a dealer at a major Japanese bank. He said this included multiple large-scale dollar buying by a Singapore bank, with each order each more than $200 million, apparently on behalf of European clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The interest rate has fallen to around 1.2% since then thanks to central banks efforts, such as the BOJ's three-month dollar-lending window offered earlier this week with the cost around 1.15% that banks tapped $4.756 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "After all, the Fed is throwing a lot of cheap dollars around the world," said Nomura's Amikura.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Japanese exporters, who need the yen toward the this fiscal year's end in March, are expected to start selling the greenback over the next few months, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'm afraid of the time when sleeping Japanese exporters who would be hungry for the yen will wake up," a trader at a major bank in Tokyo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 14-12-11 0837GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111214e7ce000he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
