\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY MARKETS-Funds dwindle in Asia as eurozone strains grow\b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2011\par 05:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  infuses dollar funds, Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * European banks' demand for ECB funds hit a two-year high\par \par \uc2 * Investors underweight towards Asian debt - StanChart\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Rising strains in the euro zone have reduced the availability of dollar funds in Asian money markets, raising the risk of a selloff in vulnerable markets like Indonesia and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Even though liquidity typically tends to recede from mid-December as banks reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  going into the year-end, traders were surprised that a noticeable drop in funding conditions began last week, underscoring fragile market sentiment.\par \par \uc2 While the funding fall hasn't led to a big selloff in Asian debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, apart from the wobble in September, a further rise in dollar costs would force foreigners to start reducing their holdings in some of these markets.\par \par \uc2 "We are a bit surprised by the drop in interbank liquidity this week," said the head of short-term rates and money markets desk at a European Bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "While most banks are comfortable holding funds this week and are loaning out money for short tenors, it does mean that raising funds is going to get a lot more difficult than we initially hoped for," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan supplied dollars in market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the fourth time this month on Tuesday - $100 million maturing in three months and $1 million maturing in a week.\par \par \uc2 The program had been untapped for more than a year because it usually costs more than borrowing dollars in the open market.\par \par \uc2 Signs of money market stress is popping up in some of the region's popular markets too. Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday by selling U.S. dollars to stabilise the local rupiah.\par \par \uc2 Such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to rise as eurozone funding conditions get worse by the day.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone banks' demand for weekly funding from the ECB hit a new two-year high on Tuesday. A total of 192 banks took a combined 265.5 billion euros at the ECB's once-a-week handout of limit-free seven-day loans.\par \par \uc2 The amount tops the 247 billion that 178 banks took last week and comes at a time when indirect gauges of securing dollar funds, such as euro/dollar cross \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basis swaps, hover near October 2008 level highs.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, one-year rates which indicates the premium that borrowers have to shell out for swapping euro interest payments to get dollars, held at 108 basis points, its most expensive level since 115 bps in late 2008.\par \par \uc2 That is dictating investor positioning towards emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 After investor meetings in the U.K, strategists at Standard Chartered Bank said most of their investors have shifted towards an underweight duration in Indonesia from overweight.\par \par \uc2 In a research note on Tuesday, the bank said investors are generally underweight in Malaysia and Thailand as Asian bond yields are unattractively low.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of how vulnerable some of these markets may be, the bank said foreign holdings in Korea are concentrated at the front end of the curve while foreign holdings in Indonesia are more concentrated at the long end of the curve.\par \par \uc2 As the cost of borrowing dollars show no signs of easing, the pressure on some of these markets are rising.\par \par \uc2 Outright three-month dollars in Singapore fixed at 0.52556 percent, its highest level since July 2010 pointing to a higher LIBOR fix later in the day.\par \par \uc2 With December end LIBOR <0#ED:> indicated at 0.6075 percent, outright rates are set to rise more in coming days.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kavita Chandran)\par \par \uc2 MONEY-MARKETS(WRAPUP 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111203e7bu0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
