\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ Shirakawa warns Japan economic outlook severe\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2011\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yen rise to weigh on exports, business sentiment\par \par \uc2 * To seek G7, G20 understanding on Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance\par \par \uc2 * Adds printing money alone won't beat deflation\par \par \uc2 * Market tension high; Europe banks struggle to raise funds\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 NAGOYA, Japan, Nov 28 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa on Monday warned the country's economy will remain in a severe state for now as Europe's sovereign debt crisis and yen rises cloud the outlook, signalling the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's readiness to boost monetary stimulus further if risks to the recovery heighten.\par \par \uc2 But he said Japan cannot escape deflation just by having the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  print money, stressing that government and corporate efforts to boost productivity and nurture new industries were also necessary.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa stuck to the view that Japan's economy will eventually resume a moderate recovery backed by solid demand in emerging nations, but warned that Europe's debt woes were the predominant risk to this outlook.\par \par \uc2 He also said Japan will continue to seek G7 and G20 understanding over its action to curb sharp yen rises, stressing that Tokyo's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at curbing excessive and disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 "When uncertainty over the overseas economic outlook is high, as is the case now, yen rises may hurt Japan's economy by reducing exports and corporate profits as well as by worsening business sentiment. We need to be mindful of this," he told business leaders in Nagoya in the central Japan prefecture of Aichi, home to automobile giant \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Japan's economy will likely be in a severe state for the time being, especially with respect to exports," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased monetary policy in October to ease the pain on the export-reliant economy from sharp yen rises and slowing overseas growth.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ kept monetary settings unchanged this month but warned of the widening fallout from Europe's debt crisis, signalling its readiness to ease policy again if Japan's economic recovery comes under threat.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said European banks are being forced to curb lending as they face difficulty raising funds in the market, warning of heightening tensions in global markets, particularly for dollar funding.\par \par \uc2 "In Europe, shrinking market confidence over its fiscal state is heightening concern over the region's financial system stability, which in turn is affecting the economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said the BOJ will continue to do its utmost to support Japan' economy. But he countered the view that it was not easing aggressively enough compared with other central banks, saying Japan's private-sector borrowing costs are the lowest among advanced economies.\par \par \uc2 "To believe that deflation can be solved by printing money alone would be considering the problem in too simplistic terms," Shirakawa said.\par \par \uc2 "What's most important is to strengthen the growth potential for Japan's economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111201e7bs00067\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal