\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOODY'S: LAUNCH OF IMF NEW CREDIT LINE POSITIVE FOR STRESSED EURO-ZONE SOVEREIGNS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund's launch of a new credit facility is positive for euro-zone sovereigns facing short-term funding problems, but can't alone solve the bloc's "escalating" crisis, Moody's Investors Service said Monday in its Weekly Credit Outlook report. (published 0537 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF SPOKESPERSON: NO DISCUSSIONS WITH ITALY ON IMF FINANCING PROGRAM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund denied Monday that it is in talks with Italy to prepare a major bailout for the Group of Seven nation. (published 0625 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: PROBABILITY OF MULTIPLE DEFAULTS BY EURO-ZONE COUNTRIES NO LONGER NEGLIGIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The probability of a series of defaults by euro-zone countries is no longer negligible, Moody's Investors Service warned Monday, noting that the longer the liquidity crisis continues, the more rapidly the risk of default would rise. (published 0451 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: INCREASING BANK RELIANCE ON ECB LIQUIDITY SUPPORT IS CREDIT NEGATIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone banks' growing reliance on financing from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is credit negative and illustrates that the crisis is weakening the credit of financial institutions, Moody's Investors Service said Monday. (published 0555 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SENIOR \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF OFFICIAL:NOT RULING OUT POSSIBILITY OF SOLO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's no reason that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be regarded as a safe-haven," given its own fiscal problems and slow pace of growth, Takehiko Nakao, Vice Minister of Finance for international affairs, said at the Paris Europlace International Financial Forum held in Tokyo. (published 0248 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: BOJ GOV SAYS JAPAN ECONOMY FACING SEVERE PRESSURES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Monday that the European debt crisis, along with the yen's continued rise, will likely put the Japanese economy in a severe situation, and he urged Europe to take bold actions to avoid a repetition of the 2008 global financial crisis. (published 0638 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVERNMENT: EXPECT INFLATION TO STABILIZE FROM 2H 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Ministry of Strategy and Finance Monday said it expects inflation to stabilize from the second half of 2012. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA 3Q OVERSEAS INVESTMENTS DOWN 34.7% ON EURO-ZONE WOES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's third-quarter total overseas investments fell sharply from a year earlier, as companies cut spending amid heightened external uncertainties stemming from the euro-zone fiscal crisis. (published 0455 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMANY WEIGHS IDEA OF CORE EURO-ZONE BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany is weighing the idea of joining with five other core euro-zone member states to issue common bonds in order to stabilize their high credit rating, German daily Die Welt reported Monday, citing sources close to the negotiations. (published 0422 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ECB NOYER BACKS EFSF LEVERAGE SCHEME FOR EURO MARKET LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Christian Noyer warned against central banks overstepping their primary mandate of maintaining mid-term price stability, with non-European central banks having gone on a public debt purchasing spree to provide liquidity to markets. (published 0522 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA VICE PREMIER: CHANCE OF LONG-TERM ECONOMIC WEAKNESS HAS RISEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chance of long-term global economic weakness has risen, Chinese Vice Premier Li Keqiang said in remarks published on a government website Monday, noting the spiralling European sovereign debt crisis, unstable international financial markets and slowing growth combined with price rises in some countries. (published 0122 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA TO RELEASE MID-YR ECONOMIC, FISCAL OUTLOOK TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government plans to release its 2011-12 Mid-Year Economic And Fiscal Outlook at 0000 GMT Tuesday. (published 0618 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: THAI INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION INDEX -35.8% ON YEAR IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's industrial production index plunged 35.8% in October compared with the same month a year earlier, as severe flooding hurt the country's manufacturing industry, the Ministry of Industry's Office of Industrial Economics said Monday. (published 0517 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI MOF: 2011 GDP GROWTH LIKELY IN 1.7%-2.0% RANGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's economic growth this year will likely be in a range of 1.7%-2.0%, much lower than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's previous forecast of 3.8%-4.3% growth in late September, mainly because of adverse effects from the country's worst floods in decades, said Somchai Sujjapongse, head of the ministry's Fiscal Policy Office. (published 0639 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PHILIPPINE GDP RISES 3.2% ON YEAR, SLOWER THAN MARKET'S 4.6% ESTIMATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine economy expanded 3.2% year-on-year in the third quarter, slower than expected due to bad weather, the higher cost of fuel, weak exports and a sluggish construction sector, raising the possibility that the country's gross domestic product will miss the government's full-year target of 4.5%-5.5% despite recent stimulus spending. (published 0536 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: THE RETURN OF THE TERMINATOR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen is back. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(11/28) EURO-ZONE SHARK STILL HAS ITS APPETITE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A shark has to constantly move forward or it dies. And I think what we got on our hands is a dead shark." Woody Allen's description of his failing relationship with Annie Hall is as apt when applied to the euro zone. Tensions within the zone are mounting as we enter a week in which Italy, Belgium, Spain and France plan to tap the markets for some 17 billion euros in new loans and, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the European economy slides into recession. (published 0531 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: KOREA NOVEMBER EXPORTS LIKELY REBOUNDED; CPI LIKELY ABOVE BOK TARGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports growth likely rebounded in November but inflation for the month likely exceeded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of 2% to 4% for the year, presenting difficult policy choices for Seoul as it faces heightened external uncertainties. (published 0449 GMT) [ 28-11-11 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111128e7bs000bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
