\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Mostly Lower As Hungary Downgrade Damps Sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 2,946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 10:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Mostly Lower As Hungary Downgrade Damps Sentiment," at 0600 GMT, misstated in the Stocks section that Italy's short-selling ban ended Friday; the ban will end in January. The error also appeared in "EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Market Prices, Data Calendar," at 0600 GMT. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen mostly lower; government debt seen lower; euro, sterling lower versus dollar; spot gold flat, oil futures lower\par \par \uc2 - ECB's Paramo: Govts Can't Expect ECB To Finance Deficits\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Downgrades Hungary Bonds To Ba1 With Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - ECB Kranjec:Euro Zone Slowdown Will Be Much Longer Than Expected\par \par \uc2 - French Finance Minister: EFSF Leverage Essentially To Protect Italy\par \par \uc2 - Schaeuble To Meet Dutch, Finnish Finance Ministers Fri-Spokesman\par \par \uc2 - EBA May Ask German Banks To Boost Capital By EUR12 Billion: Sources - Bloomberg News\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0830 GMT EuroCOIN indicator of euro area economic activity.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets are mostly lower as a continued lack of agreement between euro-zone leaders on a suitable solution to the bloc's sovereign debt crisis and a credit rating downgrade in Hungary damp sentiment.\par \par \uc2 While some buyers are coming back after a recent sell-off, trading in the region is characterized by caution ahead of the weekend, with many market participants reluctant to take major positions given the backdrop of Europe's debt problems.\par \par \uc2 Germany's Chancellor Angela Merkel Thursday again criticized calls for euro-bond issuance, after meeting with France's President Nicolas Sarkozy and Italy's Prime Minister Mario Monti, saying common interest rates for all euro-zone borrowers would send the wrong signal.\par \par \uc2 "The question still remains: when will Angela Merkel give some ground on a common Eurobond?" said Ben Le Brun, market analyst at OptionsXpress in Sydney. "With the opposition party in Germany (Thursday) even conceding that this maybe the only way forward. It certainly appears that this is a more desirable outcome than a break-up of the euro-zone members."\par \par \uc2 Australia's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index slumped to a seven-week low as selling related to the expiry of equity options contracts added to the general gloom. Banks and resources led stocks there lower.\par \par \uc2 European stocks seen mostly lower, with government debt lower. Both the euro and sterling are lower against the U.S. dollar, oil is lower, while gold is flat.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are likely to open mostly lower Friday, as investors end another week of trading centered on the euro zone's sovereign debt problems, muted by an absence of much data and U.S. markets being closed for Thanksgiving Day Thursday, and due to close early Friday.\par \par \uc2 For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down 15 at 5113, the DAX flat at 5428 and the CAC down 7 at 2815.\par \par \uc2 The outcome of Thursday's meeting of European leaders in Strasbourg will continue to attract attention.\par \par \uc2 "European leaders promised better coordination of euro-zone economic policy, in other words on medium-term decisions that produce results in the long term," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  Debt Strategist Patrick Jacq said.\par \par \uc2 "However, the market is looking for short-term solutions and essentially for a greater role for the (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). This meeting was rather disappointing," he said.\par \par \uc2 "(Germany's Chancellor) Angela Merkel continues to be dismissive of the prospect of the ECB playing a bigger role in fighting the debt crisis; it seems the German Chancellor might be saving this card for gloomier times," said Stan Shamu, market strategist at IG Markets.\par \par \uc2 ECB board member Jose Manuel Gonzalez Paramo told students at the U.K.'s Oxford University Thursday that the ECB can't be a lender of last resort to governments, and calls for it to serve in that role are motivated by self-interest.\par \par \uc2 He acknowledged that an overhaul of the E.U. Treaty "would make sense" as the current framework "would be too constraining," and said "euro bonds will exist at some point in the future. There's no doubt...(but)...at this point in time, they would not be commensurate with the degree of integration."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Moody's Investors Service downgraded Hungary's government bonds one notch to Ba1 with a negative outlook. \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services had earlier said it would maintain Hungary's BBB- credit rating on credit watch negative.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  move came after Fitch Ratings downgraded Portugal's debt to junk.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures are lower Friday. The market was closed Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro is slightly lower against the U.S. dollar amid the continued lack of concrete measures to stem the euro zone sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Disappointment that officials continue to tinker with the trivial rather than consider the bold pushed risk appetites lower and increased the downside risks to the outlook for the European sovereign-debt crisis," said Besa Deda, Chief Economist at St. George Economics.\par \par \uc2 "You should expect volatile euro trading ahead as there's much speculation going around, but what's for sure is the euro is set to fall in the longer term as the debt woes are just too far from their end," said a senior dealer at a major bank.\par \par \uc2 Investor concern toward the debt crisis has intensified after Moody's Investors Service downgraded Hungary's credit rating to Ba1, said a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. "The view that the (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has no options but to become the last lender is becoming more popular than ever."\par \par \uc2 But a Sydney-based trader said, "there are much bigger issues than Hungary; that isn't taking the euro zone down with it."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Minister of Finance Jun Azumi Friday said the ministry has been vigilantly watching for signs of speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and vowed to take various steps again if it judges it necessary to rein in the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 Some market watchers have suggested there may be more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities to kick-start growth.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan has certainly still got the market frightened that, should the old pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels be threatened, it will come in again," said Jane Foley, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "Japan has found it extremely difficult to get its own growth going," she said. "The yen is still far too strong for its exporters' liking, and it is making Japan a very hard place to turn corporate profits, so, really, what has the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  got to lose by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  further? I think there is a very strong risk that it will."\par \par \uc2 Sterling is almost flat against the euro, but nudging lower versus the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 The data calendar is thin Friday, and market attention will focus on an EUR8 billion auction of Italian six-month bills.\par \par \uc2 The sale comes after the 10-year Italian bond yield rose back above 7% Thursday--the level at which funding costs are widely considered unsustainable over the longer term--to 7.14%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in a further sign that investors are turning nervous about the euro zone as a whole, the yield on five-year U.K. government debt fell below that of Germany Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year U.K. gilt and the 10-year German bund also crossed in intraday trading, with the German benchmark hitting an intraday high of 2.265% against 2.24% for the U.K. benchmark.\par \par \uc2 "Contagion is slowly dragging some of the most secure countries into the debt crisis," said Jonathan Bristow, a broker at Valbury Capital. "If the euro-zone leaders don't turn it around soon, this global crisis will take hold on the very countries needed to get us out of it."\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said the German short end retains a strong bid, despite recent woes in the 10-year space.\par \par \uc2 German T-bills of up to 1-year are quoted with a negative offered-side yield on Tradeweb. "The bid-to offer spread is very wide but mid-points are showing some negative yields," said BarCap Director Cagdas Aksu.\par \par \uc2 The move into negative yield territory has been sparked by the markets' perception of a "more accommodative stance from the (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) with Thursday's talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be considering loans of two- or three-year maturities adding extra weight to this view" Aksu said.\par \par \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said, "The idea that participants have only now started to review their allocation of euro debt holdings is incorrect."\par \par \uc2 It points to Japanese portfolio flows, which show that with the exception of September and July, Japanese investors have reduced bund holdings every month since February.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the Belgian credit default swaps spread neared 400 basis points in volatile holiday volume due to the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday. Belgium's five-year CDS widened 26 basis points to 395 basis points, Markit said. The German spread widened 3 basis points to 112, closing in on its 116-basis-point record, although the move was in line with the European sovereign CDS index.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury futures are lower. The market was closed Thursday and will shut early Friday.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the lead December Japanese government bond futures contract is down 0.57 at 142.23. The 10-year yield is at 1.030%, up 5 basis points and topping 1% for the first time since Nov. 2.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude futures are lower in Asian trade, as a slump in U.S. crude inventories is offset by economic concerns over Europe and China.\par \par \uc2 January ICE Brent futures are down 13 cents at $107.65 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 "Oil prices remain largely range-bound with the tug-of-war between solid fundamentals and faltering macroeconomic picture continuing," said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The latest (U.S. Department of Energy) data shows a sharp fall in crude inventories...(but) the pressure from uncertainty concerning the euro area, China and global growth in general is likely to weigh on prices," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Crude "will outperform other risk assets like equities if supply conditions get worse," said a sales trader in Hong Kong. [ 25-11-11 0941GMT ]\par \par \uc2 "It will be interesting to see how (the) tug-of-war between poor demand outlook amid global economic concerns and tightening supply conditions in the Middle East will pan out," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Spot gold is at $1,691.60 a troy ounce, down 70 cents from its previous close.\par \par \uc2 Gold is locked in a narrow range around the pivotal $1,700/oz mark, and still looks vulnerable to more chart-based selling, said Credit Agricole Analyst Robin Bhar.\par \par \uc2 "That being said though, prices could still gap in either direction depending upon events in the euro zone and the fortunes of the USD, which remains the safe-haven of choice amid a flight to safety, quality and liquidity," Bhar said.\par \par \uc2 Further gold price falls toward $1,650/oz "could be on the cards," Bhar said. "Gold is under-performing as investors are more concerned about return of capital rather than the return on capital, a situation last seen during the great financial crisis of 2008-09."\par \par \uc2 Base metals are mixed on the London Metal Exchange in Asia.\par \par \uc2 LME base metals could see some pockets of buying on dips and the approach of the month end could also be supportive, while speculative shorts could be squared, Bhar said.\par \par \uc2 LME three-month copper is at $7,264 a metric ton, down $1 and moving in and out of positive territory, trading in a very narrow band. Aluminum is at $2,031.50/ton, up $13.\par \par \uc2 -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Christopher Cushing contributed to this report. [ 25-11-11 0941GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA          Close \par Nasdaq        Close \par S&P 500       Close \par FTSE 100      5127.57    -12.21   -0.24% \par Xetra DAX     5428.11    -29.66   -0.54% \par CAC40         2822.25    -0.18    -0.01% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225    8181.91    +16.73  +0.20% \par Hang Seng     17704.54   -230.56 -1.29% \par S&P/ASX 200   3984.80    -59.40  -1.47% \par Taiwan Index  6812.52    -51.87  -0.76% \par S.Korea Kospi 1770.54    -24.52  -1.37% \par Shanghai Comp 2388.86    -8.69   -0.36% \par Bombay SenSex 15843.52   -14.97  -0.09% \par Dow Future    11192.00   -42.00  -0.4% \par NASDAQ Futur