\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 01:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 US market closed for holiday thursday. The EUR/USD is little changed after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut Hungary's rating to Ba1 with a negative outlook. There are much bigger issues than Hungary; that isn't taking the euro zone down with it. The EUR/USD is at 1.3346, up slightly from 1.3320 in late Canada trading.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias as investors disappointed over lack of positive news from yesterday's 3-way meeting between German, French and Italian leaders: German Chancellor Merkel stood by her refusal to widen ECB's role to support weaker euro-zone states and dashed hopes that she was warming up to idea of joint euro-zone bonds, signaling there's no agreement on how to resolve long-running debt crisis. EUR/USD also undermined by growing fears of breakup of the euro zone; Fitch Ratings' downgrade of Portugal's sovereign credit rating to junk and reports that troubled French-Belgian bank \uc2 Dexia\uc2  was tapping emergency liquidity assistance; \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of Hungary's rating to Ba1 with negative outlook; Italian 10-year yields rising above critical 7% level (last 7.097%); dollar-funding pressures facing euro-zone banks (premium for 3-month EUR/USD swap stays elevated at around 15 pips); fears of euro-zone recession; expectations of rate cut from ECB in coming months. But euro sentiment soothed by surprise rise in German November Ifo business climate index to 106.6 from 106.4 in October (vs forecast for fall to 105.1). EUR/USD losses also tempered by sovereign demand for euro; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0745 GMT France November consumer confidence survey, 0830 GMT October EuroCOIN indicator of euro area economic activity. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.3316 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3241 (Oct. 6 low), then 1.3145 (Oct. 4 swing low), psychological 1.3000 line and 1.2860 (Jan. 10 swing low). Resistance at 1.3412 (yesterday's high), then at 1.3481 (hourly chart) and 1.3532 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.3569 (Tuesday's high), then 1.3615 (Nov. 18 high), 1.3641 (Nov. 15 high) and 1.3815 (Nov. 14 high).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to range-trade. Undermined by unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades as risk sentiment stays fragile, European stocks fell modestly yesterday (German DAX drop 0.5%, UK FTSE shed 0.2%) amid ongoing European sovereign debt crisis and worries over health of global economy. USD/JPY also weighed by Japan exporter sales. BUT USD/JPY downside limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand, expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , broad USD strength as contagion from euro-zone crisis hits Asian markets; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 2330 GMT Japan October nation CPI, November Tokyo CPI, 2350 GMT Japan October corporate service price index; U.S. stock market close at 1 pm NY Time. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode; inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 77.33 (yesterday's high), then at 77.58 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 77.69 (Nov. 11 high), then 77.88 (Nov. 10 high), 78.10 (Nov. 8 high), 78.22-78.27 band (Nov. 7 high-Nov. 4 high) and 78.41 (Nov. 2 high). Support at 77.00-76.92 band (yesterday's low-Wednesday's low), then at 76.83-76.73 band (Tuesday's low-Monday's low) and 76.57 (Nov. 18 low); breach would target 76.31 (76.4% Fibonacci correction of Oct. 31's 75.31-79.55 spike), then 75.31 (Oct. 31 record low) and psychological 75.00 level.\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by weak investor risk appetite; expectations of more quantitative easing from Bank of England MPC in coming months. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on buoyant EUR/GBP cross as UK attracts safe-haven flows from investors fleeing European crisis; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 1200 GMT BOE's Martin Weale speaks. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.5482 (yesterday's low); breach would target 1.5421 (Oct. 7 low), then 1.5270 (Oct. 6 swing low), 1.5123 (July 21, 2010 low), psychological 1.5000 line and 1.4946 (July 12, 2010 low). Resistance at 1.5565 (yesterday's high), then at 1.5654 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5691 (Tuesday's high), then 1.5796 (Monday's high), 1.5888 (Nov. 18 high) and 1.5931 (Nov. 15 high).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with bullish bias. Buoyed by broadly firmer USD undertone as investor risk appetite stays subdued; expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But USD/CHF gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics stays elevated at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 0.9215-0.9222 band (yesterday's high-Nov. 18 high), then at 0.9235 (Nov. 17 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9290 (Oct. 10 high), then 0.9317 (Oct. 6 high), 0.9340 (April 1 reaction high) and 0.9369 (March 9 reaction high). Support at 0.9150 (yesterday's low), then at 0.9123 (Wednesday's low), 0.9104 (Tuesday's low) and 0.9081 (Nov. 18 low); breach would expose downside to 0.8982 (55-day moving average), then 0.8958-0.8950 band (Nov. 14 low-Nov. 11 low), 0.8918 (Nov. 8 low) and 0.8865 (Nov. 7 low).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by reduced investor risk appetite; expectations of rate cut from RBA in coming months as global growth outlook darkens; Aussie sales on softer AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; though inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.9675 (hourly chart), then at 0.9659 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9617 (Oct. 6 low), then 0.9484 (Oct. 5 low) and 0.9386 (Oct. 4 swing low). Resistance at 0.9784 (yesterday's high), then at 0.9855 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9898 (Tuesday's high), then 1.0014 (Monday's high), 1.0117 (Nov. 17 high) and 1.0184 (Nov. 16 high).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by decreased investor risk appetite. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap; Kiwi demand on softer AUD/NZD cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Focus on New Zealand general election Saturday with recent polls suggesting PM John Key could return to power with enough support to govern alone. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.7381 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7288 (March 21 low), then 0.7113 (March 17 swing low). Resistance at 0.7454 (yesterday's high), then at 0.7503-0.7513 band (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7575 (Monday's high), then 0.7649 (Nov. 18 high), 0.7687 (Nov. 17 high) and 0.7723 (Nov. 16 high).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar Thursday after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower through its daily reference rate. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3680 around 0830 GMT, up from CNY6.3590 late Wednesday. It traded in a range of CNY6.3563 to CNY6.3731. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3570 Thursday, up from 6.3498 Wednesday. Traders said the dollar is likely to continue to trade in a range of CNY6.3500 to CNY6.3700 in the near term. One-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.3890/6.3940 from 6.3690/6.3730 late Wednesday, implying a 0.4% fall by the yuan over the next year. In Hong Kong, where the yuan floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was 6.3905, up from 6.3775 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111125e7bp00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
