\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan's Azumi wary of call for BOJ to buy foreign bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2011\par 02:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Former BOJ Gov called for Y50 trln of foreign bond buying\par \par \uc2 * Proposal runs counter to govt stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -finmin\par \par \uc2 * BOJ, govt cautious about proposed foreign bond purchases\par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, background)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 22 (Reuters) - It would be hard for Japan's government to accept a call for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy a massive amount of foreign bonds to stem the yen's rise, Finance Minister Jun Azumi said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Former Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Kazumasa Iwata has proposed setting up a crisis prevention facility to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase 50 trillion yen ($650 billion) of foreign bonds such as those issued by the European Financial Stability Facility (EFSF).\par \par \uc2 But the proposal was not in line with the government stance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent speculative and disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, Azumi said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wary over any incursions into its jurisdiction over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ is reluctant to boost its balance sheet, arguing it is already taking bold steps. Iwata called on the ministry to shoulder any incurred losses.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda has said, however, that the idea would be discussed at the national strategy council, made up of ministers and business leaders.\par \par \uc2 By law, the government has no right to print cash and has to fund its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by issuing financing bills (FBs), usually of three-month duration. It sets the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount and the BOJ, which has the right to print cash, acts as its agent.\par \par \uc2 "If (the BOJ) was to buy 50 trillion yen of foreign-denominated bonds, that would require boosting (of government issuance) of FBs by 50 trillion yen to raise yen funds to buy foreign bonds," Azumi said.\par \par \uc2 "That would effectively end up being \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it would be different from the policy that we have pursued."\par \par \uc2 Azumi's comments sent the dollar as high as 77.35 yen from around 76.90 yen as some traders initially took them as hinting at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback quickly pared its gains though as the full context of Azumi's comments emerged.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at around 77 versus the yen, above a record low of 75.31 hit just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct. 31, estimated at a daily record of 7.7 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 The government has launched a credit facility by utilising its $1.2 trillion foreign reserves, with the aim of encouraging Japanese firms to take advantage of their robust \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to acquire overseas companies and to buy resource assets.\par \par \uc2 But some ruling party lawmakers have been calling for creating a fund aimed at purchasing foreign bonds to curb the yen's rise that threatens exports and drives Japanese production and job opportunities overseas.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.9800 Japanese yen)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/AZUMI(UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111125e7bm0002o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
