\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 868 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2011\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN 3Q GDP GREW IN LINE WITH EXPECTATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)-German economic growth accelerated in the third quarter of this year in line with expectations, data from the Federal Statistics Office, Destatis, showed Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH ECONOMY RETURNS TO GROWTH IN THIRD QUARTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French economy returned to growth in the third quarter after contracting in the second, data from national statistics agency Insee showed Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PM NODA: WILL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF EXCESSIVE MOVES IN THE YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda issued warnings about the rebounding yen Tuesday, saying the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rises in a way that looks "excessive" to him.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN AZUMI: EXPECTS BOJ TO TAKE PROPER STEPS IN DEALING WITH YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister voiced hope Tuesday that the Bank of Japan would continue its efforts to tackle the yen's strength, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gradually regains ground lost after last month's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Tokyo authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRIAN 3Q GDP +0.3% VS 2Q, +2.6% ON YEAR-WIFO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 VIENNA (Dow Jones)--Austrian economic growth continued to slow in the third quarter after a significant slowdown in the second quarter, the country's main economic institute WIFO said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TURKEY SEPT CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT $6.8B VERSUS $4B IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Turkey's current account deficit widened on the month in line with forecasts in September, underscoring investor nerves over the fast-growing economy's exposure to shocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA RBA SAYS NEUTRAL POLICY BEST FOR SUSTAINED GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday its decision to cut interest rates at the start of the month reflected a desire for a more neutral monetary policy, but signaled that the effect of an ongoing mining boom still posed the risk of needing to have interest rates back at a more restrictive level\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: IMF: CHINA BANKING SYSTEM NEEDS TO RELY MORE ON MARKET MECHANISMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China needs to revamp its banking system so that it relies more on market mechanisms, such as interest rates, to head off a "steady build-up of financial sector vulnerabilities," the International Monetary Fund said, in its first broad review of China's financial system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA TO SPEED UP 1H 2012 SPENDING AMID GROWTH WORRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan said Tuesday the government will likely speed up budget spending in the first half of 2012, because it expects tougher growth conditions during that period than in the second half, highlighting increasing concerns about slowing growth momentum in Asia's fourth-largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER: ASIAN COUNTRIES NEED CURBS TO COUNTERACT SPECULATIVE CAPITAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Tuesday said regulatory measures to curb the negative impact of rapid capital flows is crucial for Asian economies to counteract speculation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU TO SET OUT LEGISLATION TO CURB RATING AGENCIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe Tuesday sets out long-awaited proposals to regulate credit rating agencies whose assessments are considered to have played a key role in the sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S APRA TO RELEASE REVISED LIQUIDITY STANDARDS WEDNESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's prudential regulator said Tuesday it plans to release a revised proposal of liquidity standards on Wednesday as the country moves to meet new global banking regulations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: TAIWAN TO EASE RULE FOR INSURERS ON OVERSEAS INVESTMENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan plans to allow insurance companies to exclude premium income from foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies from being counted as part of their overseas investment portfolios, a change that will increase the funds that insurers can deploy abroad to seek better returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANK OF THAILAND\f28 \uc2 \f2 : 4Q LENDING GROWTH TO SLOW ON FLOOD, GLOBAL CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday that lending growth in the final quarter will likely be slower than the third quarter's 17.3% year-on-year rise, due mainly to gloomy global economic conditions and the adverse effect on the local economy of Thailand's severe floods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: 2012 INFLATION LIKELY 4.7% VS 4.2% FORECAST IN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia expects 2012 inflation to come in at 4.7%, faster than a forecast of 4.2% for this year, Director of Research for Economic and Monetary Policies at the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Perry Warjiyo told reporters Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: PHILIPPINES SEPT OVERSEAS WORKERS REMITTANCES $1.735 BLN, +8.4% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Filipinos working overseas sent home $1.735 billion in September--the second-largest amount for a single month, up 8.4% from the $1.60 billion posted in the same period last year, helped by a steady increase in the numbers of workers deployed abroad, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJE(11/15)HEARD ON THE ST:ECB WIELDING BAZOOKA MAY THREATEN EURO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European politicians argue tirelessly that the euro isn't in crisis, and so far the markets seem to believe them. At $1.3622, the euro is up 1.7% against the dollar year-to-date and far above lows of $1.20 hit in June 2010 after the euro-zone debt crisis first exploded. [ 15-11-11 0903GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111115e7bf000m7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
