\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Asian Markets Rise On Positive Europe Sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 1,045 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2011\par 04:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA          12153.68 +259.89  +2.19% \par Nasdaq        2678.75  +53.60   +2.04% \par S&P 500       1263.85  +24.16   +1.95% \par FTSE 100      5545.38  +100.56  +1.85% \par Nikkei Stock  8643.04  +128.57  +1.51% \par Hang Seng     19559.55 +422.38  +2.21%  - \par Kospi         1899.83  +36.38   +1.95% \par  \par USD/JPY    77.16-22    -0.15% \par Range      77.19-77.12 \par EUR/USD    1.3791-96  +99.10% \par Range      1.3814-1.3755 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec    $6.166  per bushel \par Comex Gold        $1,788.10  +$28.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)  $98.99      +$1.21 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks soared Friday, capping a volatile week, as signs of stabilization in Europe and better-than-expected consumer sentiment data fueled a second straight day of big gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  led the blue chips higher, rising $2.06, or 6%, to 36.70. The media company's fiscal fourth-quarter profit jumped 30%, boosted by strength in its theme-parks and television business segments. All 30 Dow components rose Friday.\par \par \uc2 Friday's rally capped a week of alternating bouts of upbeat and depressing news regarding Europe's sovereign-debt crisis. Investors were calmed toward the end of the week as a changing of the guard among Italian and Greek political leadership soothed some fears.\par \par \uc2 Still, a lack of concrete evidence regarding a true resolution to Europe's debt woes has left investors nervous about what looms ahead.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were higher Monday on some signs of stability in Europe, while a positive third-quarter economic growth reading boosted sentiment in Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets showed some relief on the appointment of economist and former European Union competition commissioner Mario Monti as Italy's premier-designate Sunday with investors viewing the development as a step toward bringing Europe's sovereign-debt crisis under control.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, many traders cautioned against getting too carried away.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are thinking these political developments will help Europe find a way out of its debt crisis," said BBY senior institutional trader Peter Copeland. "But this bullishness won't be widely embraced as European bond markets remain fragile. A lot of people will see these developments as just kicking the can down the road."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 63 points in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in Tokyo took heart from Japan's third-quarter gross domestic product, which rose for the first time since the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. Third-quarter real GDP swelled at an annualized pace of 6.0%, edging out expectations for 5.9% growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While there are concerns of an economic slowdown across the globe, there is some temporary relief that Japan's July-September GDP is better than other developed economies," says Yoshinori Nagano, a senior strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 2.9% helped by an announcement Friday that a group spearheaded by the company's music division will buy the publishing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \uc2 \f2 EMI Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $2.2 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cyclicals dominated broad-based gains in the region. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sharp Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . rose 1.0%, and Inpex advanced 1.1% in Tokyo, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 2.2% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  tacked on 1.6% in Seoul. Australia's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Woodside Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Origin Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 1.5%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  advanced 1.4%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was roughly steady against the U.S. dollar as investors remained circumspect about the sovereign-debt situation in Europe despite the appointment of a new leader in Italy over the weekend and in Greece last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Markets are likely to be cautious in their enthusiasm for the new governments at this stage. Both have a fragile hold on political power and their capacity to implement the kinds of reform needed to restore market confidence is yet to be tested," said Ric Spooner Chief Markets Analyst at CMC Markets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was at $1.3768 against the dollar, from $1.3755 late Monday in New York, and at Y106.12 against the yen, from Y106.40. The dollar was at Y77.16, from Y77.14.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,790.90 per troy ounce, up $2.40 from its New York settlement on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December Nymex crude oil futures were up six cents at $99.05 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par Monti Asked To Form Government, Says Italy Can Overcome Crisis \par Obama Pressures China's Hu On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Rebalancing \par China's Hu: Even Big Rise In Yuan Can't Resolve U.S Problems - Xinhua \par Obama, Leaders Announce Outlines Of Trans-Pacific Trade Pact \par Berlusconi's Party To Support 'Technical' Monti Govt \par ECB Weidmann: ECB Not Lender Of Last Resort For Governments -FT \par France,Germany To New Greek Prime Minister:Must Honor Commitments \par China To US: Yuan Is At 'Fair-Value'--Xinhua \par ECB Orphanides: Greek Debt Write-down Harmful To Euro Zone-Paper \par German Govt Prepares For Greece To Exit Euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Union - Report \par China Urges More Trade With Southeast-Asian Nations \par Japan's Free-Trade Move Criticized \par Swiss Economy Minister Warns Against Pushing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  To Weaken Franc \par Greeks Welcome Papademos, Coalition Government - Polls \par Japan, China Agree To Join Forces On Global Financial Challenges \par Malaysian PM Najib: Won't Invest In European Rescue \par INTERVIEW: Thai Deputy PM: Determined To Stay As Southeast Asia's Industrial Hub \par IBM, Chinese Company Suning Appliance In E-Commerce Pact \par Singapore Prime Minister: Growth Likely Slow Over Next Few Quarters \par WSJ UPDATE: IMF Head Suggests \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Justified \par Singapore Sovereign Fund Sold Most Of Olympus Stake \par Libya NOC Head Sees Oil Output At Pre-War Level By End '12 \par Arab League Suspends Syria, Effective Wed \par NZ 3Q Real Retail Sales +2.2% Vs 2Q; Consensus +0.6% \par Greece Names New Cabinet \par Obama Warns Budget Supercommittee, Urges Action \par SEC Employees Disciplined Over Madoff Case \par S&P Says Computer Glitch Led To Erroneous Message \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sale Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 EMI Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  Nears Conclusion \par Reuters/Univ Michigan Mid-Nov Sentiment Climbs \par More Than 1,000 \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  Staff Fired \par Mexican Interior Minister Killed In Crash \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 14-11-11 0303GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020111114e7be00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\p