\par\par \uc2 Forex Focus\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Curbing Yen Strength Will Get More Difficult\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2011\par 12:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 -- Impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will diminish\par \par \uc2 -- JPY Inflows likely to intensify with debt crisis\par \par \uc2 -- Yield spreads undermine USD support\par \par \uc2 LONDON (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--Curbing yen strength will only get more difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not only is the impact of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to diminish, now that many long yen positions have been reduced, but upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely intensify as the euro-zone debt crisis continues to deteriorate.\par \par \uc2 As the Japanese authorities have already learnt, conducting successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is difficult at the best of times.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's Y7.7 trillion exercise at the end of last month did have some initial success, driving the dollar back over Y79 from under Y76.\par \par \uc2 According to reports in Tokyo, which actually have been denied by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a further Y900 billion or so was spent since then on stealth or covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued buying dollars against the yen but without going public. This has helped to keep the dollar from sliding back under Y77.00.\par \par \uc2 But it is only a matter of time before the dollar falls back under that level.\par \par \uc2 For a start, Japan's attempts to curb the strength of the yen aren't attracting any international support.\par \par \uc2 On the contrary, with the U.S. trying to get China to stop manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and with so many global economies hoping that exports will save them from another recession, Japan is very much on its own.\par \par \uc2 And, as history has made clear, unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercises are seldom as convincing or as successful as those coordinated with other nations.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the issue of positioning. The surprise attack by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  on Oct. 31 will have chased many of those holding long speculative yen positions out of the market for now. This means that there will be less leverage for future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen will be less responsive.\par \par \uc2 Of equal importance are the growing investment inflows into the yen.\par \par \uc2 The recent sharp rise in Italian bond yields and growing fears of a downgrade in France's credit rating means that the yen's recent role as a safe haven is only likely to expand. There are already signs from weekly securities data that Japanese investors themselves are bringing more funds back home, back into yen, as the euro-zone debt crisis refuses to go away.\par \par \uc2 Japan's status as a current-account surplus country is also unlikely to go away, ensuring that underlying trade flows remain in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's favor.\par \par \uc2 But of particular importance in the yen's rise against the dollar is the sharp narrowing in the U.S.' two-year bond yield advantage over Japan, which has fallen to only 10 basis points in recent weeks and further reduces the dollar's attraction while expectations of monetary tightening in the U.S. remain so low.\par \par \uc2 So, despite the continuing high cost to the Japanese economy of a strong yen, and the likely return of deflation fears as import prices are depressed, there is little that the Japanese authorities can do just now.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 11, 2011 06:25 ET (11:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120111111e7bb001lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
