\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Machinery Orders Fall 8.2% in September\b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2011\par 01:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Machinery orders fall 8.2% in September from previous month\par \par \uc2 -- Worse than 7.1% fall expected by market\par \par \uc2 -- Data suggests orders tapering off after surge from post-quake reconstruction\par \par \uc2 (Adds further detail, remarks from briefing official, background, 5th-8th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By George Nishiyama \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese core machinery orders fell 8.2% in September from a month earlier, the government said Thursday, as orders tapered off amid the strong yen after a surge in August on the back of post-quake reconstruction demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fall in machinery orders, regarded as a leading indicator of corporate capital investment, was deeper than the median forecast for a 7.1% decline in a poll of economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and the Nikkei.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unadjusted core orders in September rose 9.8% from a year ago, while orders rose 1.5% in the July-September period from the previous quarter, the Cabinet Office said. It said core orders are expected to fall 3.8% in the October-December quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The decline, the first in two months, confirms other recent data suggesting that just as Japan's economy has been recovering from the severe disruptions caused by the mid-March earthquake, it has been hit by global uncertainty and signs of slowing growth in key markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Cabinet Office official briefing reporters said the result showed "one step forward, one step back" for Japanese machinery orders, adding that the strong yen was one reason for the fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The result prompted the Cabinet Office to downgrade its assessment of machinery orders for the first time since November 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's periodic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the yen remains stubbornly strong against other key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as international investors look for a safe haven amid turmoil in the U.S. and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday that Japan's current account surplus fell for the seventh-straight month in September. More worryingly for the export sector other data released Wednesday showed Japan's trade of goods balance was in deficit for the first 20 days of October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While often volatile, the partial October figures reinforce recent views that, although Japan's current economic recovery will likely continue in the months ahead, the momentum could weaken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Core orders exclude those from electric power companies and those for ships, which are often a source of volatility in the overall data due to their large sizes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By George Nishiyama, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com [ 10-11-11 0040GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111110e7ba0001b\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or cu